OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026", "title": "Polynomial vacuum representations and bridge mass for honeycomb walks", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "237", "family_title": "The three-quarter exponent for honeycomb self-avoiding walk", "abstract_html": "We prove that, under the all-length critical measure, a honeycomb bridge crossing a strip of <i>h</i> layers, with its initial port fixed and its terminal port free, has mean length \\(h^{4/3+o(1)}\\). We also obtain central visit probability \\(R^{-2/3+o(1)}\\) and mean length \\(R^{4/3+o(1)}\\) for macroscopic free-boundary chords in a regular hexagon.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Strip mass and bridge length)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "As integer \\(h\\to\\infty\\), \\( b_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad \\ell_h=h^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad \\mathbb E_h|\\gamma|=h^{4/3+o(1)}. \\) All three sums are finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the honeycomb lattice dual to the equilateral triangular tiling of side \\(1\\). Honeycomb edges have length \\(1/\\sqrt3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A port is the midpoint of an edge cut by a tiling line. A path between ports visits distinct honeycomb vertices. Its length \\(|\\gamma|\\) is the number of visited vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Each visited vertex has critical weight\n  \\[\n  x_*=(2\\cos(\\pi/8))^{-1}=(2+\\sqrt2)^{-1/2};\n  \\]\n  terminal half-edges have no additional weight.</p><p class=\"li\">A strip of \\(h\\) layers has physical height \\(h\\sqrt3/2\\). Fix the bottom source at horizontal coordinate \\(0\\); attainable top ports have coordinates in \\(\\mathbb Z+h/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B_h(e)\\) consist of paths confined to the strip from this source to the top port \\(e\\), and define\n  \\[\n  b_h(e)=\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}x_*^{|\\gamma|},\n  \\qquad\n  b_h=\\sum_e b_h(e),\n  \\qquad\n  \\ell_h=\\sum_e\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h(e)}\n                  |\\gamma|x_*^{|\\gamma|}.\n  \\]\n  The bridge law assigns probability \\(x_*^{|\\gamma|}/b_h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(f(h)=h^{a+o(1)}\\) means eventual positivity and\n  \\(\\log f(h)/\\log h\\to a\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As integer \\(h\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nb_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\ell_h=h^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_h|\\gamma|=h^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nAll three sums are finite. These exponents are unchanged if paths are additionally confined to horizontal distance \\(h\\log^2h\\) from the initial port. The conclusions have subpower precision and do not assert comparability by fixed constants.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\nb_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(v\\) in the central third, and use \\(m(v),u(v),J,J_{CC}\\) from Proposition 18. Proposition 19 gives\n\\[\nJ\\ge h^{3/4-o(1)}u(v).\n\\]\nCombine with Proposition 18:\n\\[\nJ\\le C(J_{CC}+m(v)),\n\\qquad\nJ_{CC}\\le h^{3/4+o(1)}m(v)+O(h^{-100}).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nu(v)\\le h^{o(1)}m(v)\n+O(h^{-100-3/4+o(1)}).\n\\]</p><p>Ordered excursions count each path twice, so\n\\[\n2(u(v)+m(v))=A_{S_h}(v).\n\\]\nProposition 17 gives \\(u(v)+m(v)=h^{1/12+o(1)}\\) uniformly in the central third. The additive error cannot carry this mass. Therefore\n\\[\nm(v)=h^{1/12+o(1)}\n\\]\nthere, and \\(m(v)\\le h^{1/12+o(1)}\\) throughout the strip.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal V_h\\) contain one representative of each horizontal-translation orbit of strip vertices. Translating a bridge's bottom source to the fixed source identifies \\(m(v)\\) with weighted visits to that orbit. Exactly,\n\\[\n\\ell_h=\\sum_{v\\in\\mathcal V_h}m(v).\n\\]\nThere are \\(O(h)\\) orbits, with order \\(h\\) represented in the central third. Thus\n\\[\n\\ell_h=h^{13/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nDivision gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_h|\\gamma|\n=\\ell_h/b_h\n=h^{13/12+1/4+o(1)}\n=h^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Propositions 1 and 15 give finiteness and show that the discarded mass and first moment at lateral range \\(h\\log^2h\\) are negligible. The same exponents therefore hold with that confinement.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2 (Hexagon visits)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The normalizing mass is \\(R^{3/4+o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the lattice, port convention, visited-vertex length, and critical weight specified in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">In a regular lattice hexagon of integer side \\(R\\), sum \\(x_*^{|\\gamma|}\\) over paths joining ordered distinct boundary ports, retaining only paths of diameter at least \\(R/10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Reversal gives a second ordered path. Let \\(\\mathbb P_R\\) be the normalized positive law and \\(\\mathbb E_R\\) its expectation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalizing mass is \\(R^{3/4+o(1)}\\). Uniformly at vertices in the concentric hexagon of side \\(R/3\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_R(v\\in\\gamma)=R^{-2/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe corresponding upper bound holds at every vertex, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_R|\\gamma|=R^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 16,\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_R=R^{3/4+o(1)},\n\\qquad\nV_R(v)=R^{1/12+o(1)}\n\\]\nuniformly in the central third, with the corresponding global upper bound. Dividing yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_R(v\\in\\gamma)\n=R^{1/12-3/4+o(1)}\n=R^{-2/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe same upper exponent holds at every vertex.</p><p>There are order \\(R^2\\) vertices in the central third and \\(O(R^2)\\) in the whole hexagon. Since length counts visited vertices,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_R|\\gamma|\n=\\sum_v\\mathbb P_R(v\\in\\gamma)\n=R^{2-2/3+o(1)}\n=R^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Proposition 1.3 (Mixed exits), retained here as a headline result", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The retained event has mass \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\) and first-length mass \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the critical honeycomb path convention of Theorem 1 in the full planar strip of \\(h\\) layers.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix one bottom source. An arch returns to the bottom wall; a bridge exits on the top wall.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\vartheta&lt;1/2\\), and retain arches and bridges reaching at least \\(\\vartheta h\\) layers above their starting wall.</p><p class=\"li\">Mass means the unnormalized sum of \\(x_*^{|\\gamma|}\\); first-length mass means the sum of \\(|\\gamma|x_*^{|\\gamma|}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The retained event has mass \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\) and first-length mass \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\). Its normalized mean length is therefore \\(h^{4/3+o(1)}\\).</p><p>Restricting horizontal distance to \\(h\\log^2h\\) preserves these exponents. Without the height condition, the total first-length mass of all exits is at most \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 17 gives conditioned mass \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\), conditioned first-length mass \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\), and the stated unconditioned upper bound. Their ratio is\n\\[\nh^{13/12+1/4+o(1)}=h^{4/3+o(1)}.\n\\]\nProposition 15 makes removal of horizontal range \\(h\\log^2h\\) negligible for both relevant sums.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Theorem 1.4 (Normalized separator pairing)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every compact \\(K\\subset\\{s\\in\\mathbb C:|\\Im s|<\\pi\\}\\), there is \\(C_K<\\infty\\) such that \\( |F_n(2\\cosh s)| \\le C_Kn^{1/6+2\\Re(s^2)/(3\\pi^2)} \\qquad(n\\ge1,\\ s\\in K). \\) There is a disk about \\(\\eta=0\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Identify horizontal translates by \\(2n\\), obtaining an infinite honeycomb cylinder.</p><p class=\"li\">Mark two face centers on one horizontal cut, separated by \\(n\\) sites.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_n(\\eta)\\) sum over systems of pairwise vertex-disjoint unoriented simple polygons, each separating the two marks. A polygon \\(P\\) has weight \\(\\eta x_*^{|P|}\\), and the empty system has weight \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each separating polygon crosses the marked interval. Thus \\(F_n\\) is a polynomial in \\(\\eta\\), with convergent weighted coefficients.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every compact \\(K\\subset\\{s\\in\\mathbb C:|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\}\\), there is \\(C_K&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n|F_n(2\\cosh s)|\n\\le C_Kn^{1/6+2\\Re(s^2)/(3\\pi^2)}\n\\qquad(n\\ge1,\\ s\\in K).\n\\]\nThere is a disk about \\(\\eta=0\\), independent of \\(n\\), in which \\(F_n\\) has no zeros. For every fixed \\(k\\), the order-\\(k\\) Taylor coefficient of \\(\\log F_n\\) at \\(0\\) is \\(O_k(1+\\log n)\\). Finally,\n\\[\nF_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 identifies\n\\[\nF_n(2\\cosh s)=X_{n,n}(e^s)\n\\]\nwith its positive physical polygon polynomial. Proposition 7 supplies the finite numerator formula and small-cycle representation; Proposition 8 fixes the homogeneous Pfaffian denominator. Propositions 9–11 perform radial deformation, winding-sensitive upper resummation, residual-kernel control, absolute summation, and bulk calibration. They give\n\\[\n|F_n(2\\cosh s)|\n\\le C_Kn^{1/6+2\\Re(s^2)/(3\\pi^2)}\n\\]\nuniformly on each compact subset of \\(|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\).</p><p>Proposition 12 uses the exact normalization at \\(s=i\\pi/2\\), analytic local boundedness, and coefficient positivity to obtain the common zero-free disk, the fixed-order logarithmic coefficient bounds, and\n\\[\nF_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite convex-domain flux identity, strip normalization and crude bridge estimates, and fixed-width cylindrical convergence are those of Lemmas 1.1–1.3 below.", "statement": "<p>Planar flux, criticality, and fixed-width convergence.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the honeycomb lattice and visited-vertex convention of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=\\pi/8,\\qquad \\sigma=3/8,\\qquad x_*=(2\\cos\\lambda)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Walks are simple, cannot immediately reverse an edge, and stop at the port just before leaving the allowed triangles.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_h\\) and \\(b_h\\) be the nontrivial bottom-to-bottom and bottom-to-top masses from a fixed bottom port in an \\(h\\)-layer strip.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite convex-domain flux identity, strip normalization and crude bridge estimates, and fixed-width cylindrical convergence are those of Lemmas 1.1–1.3 below. In addition, \\(x_*\\) is the critical vertex weight: the connective constant is \\(1/x_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.3 give the stated flux and convergence assertions.</p><p>For criticality, a positive-span vertex-to-vertex weak bridge can be extended to bracketing layer cuts using strictly more extreme vertices, at most one per endpoint. Lemma 1.2 therefore bounds its weighted mass uniformly in the span.</p><p>Split a length-\\(L\\) vertex walk at a lowest vertex. On each weak half-space half, cut successively at the last maximum, the last minimum of the remainder, and so on. The resulting alternating weak bridges have decreasing positive spans, with the first two possibly equal, measured in thirds of a layer. Their span sum is at most \\(2L\\) on each half. There are \\(O(\\sqrt L)\\) pieces and\n\\[\n\\exp(O(\\sqrt L\\log(L+1)))\n\\]\nspan lists. Two translation types suffice, and the final translation to the original start is fixed. Multiplying uniform bridge bounds and a bounded factor per joint shows that the exponential walk-count rate is at most \\(1/x_*\\).</p><p>A strictly smaller rate would make critical vertex susceptibility finite and, after trimming port half-edges, would imply \\(\\sum_hb_h&lt;\\infty\\), contradicting Lemma 1.2. Existence of the rate follows from submultiplicativity and lattice transitivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Critical flux)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For nontrivial exits, \\( \\sum_\\gamma x_*^{|\\gamma|}e^{i\\sigma W(\\gamma)}=1. \\) Every such walk satisfies \\(|W(\\gamma)|\\le\\pi\\), so …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a finite convex domain bounded by tiling lines.</p><p class=\"li\">Start a path inward from an outer boundary port and let it exit at any boundary port.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W(\\gamma)\\) be its total turning angle.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For nontrivial exits,\n\\[\n\\sum_\\gamma x_*^{|\\gamma|}e^{i\\sigma W(\\gamma)}=1.\n\\]\nEvery such walk satisfies \\(|W(\\gamma)|\\le\\pi\\), so\n\\[\n\\Re\\!\\left(x_*^{|\\gamma|}e^{i\\sigma W(\\gamma)}\\right)\n\\ge\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)x_*^{|\\gamma|}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At each interior vertex, sum phased path weights to its adjacent ports, with sign \\(+\\) when pointing away from that vertex and sign \\(-\\) when pointing toward it. The initial empty path points toward the interior. Summing over vertices cancels internal terms.</p><p>At an unused vertex, the two extensions balance the incoming term because\n\\[\n2x_*\\cos(\\sigma\\pi/3)=1.\n\\]\nThe remaining negative terms have previously used the vertex. Their tails traverse one of the two orientations of a polygon attached to a stem. Reverse the traversal after the stem; this changes the terminal port but preserves the number of vertices. The stem lies outside the polygon. Relative to the end of the stem, the additional turns are\n\\[\n\\pm(2\\pi-2\\pi/3)=\\pm4\\pi/3,\n\\]\nwhose phases cancel.</p><p>A simple exit arc in a convex polygon can be closed by the counterclockwise boundary arc. Subtracting the intervening boundary turns and the two right angles from \\(2\\pi\\) gives \\(|W|\\le\\pi\\), proving the real-part comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Corollary 2.2 (Strip flux and crude bridge bounds)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The masses are finite and \\( b_h+\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)A_h=1. \\) Moreover, for an absolute \\(c>0\\), \\( A_{h+1}-A_h\\le x_*^{-1}b_{h+1}^2, \\qquad \\frac{c}{h+1}\\le b_h\\le1. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(A_h,b_h\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Bottom arches to the right have turn \\(-\\pi\\), those to the left turn \\(\\pi\\), and opposite-side exits turn \\(0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The masses are finite and\n\\[\nb_h+\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)A_h=1. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nMoreover, for an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nA_{h+1}-A_h\\le x_*^{-1}b_{h+1}^2,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{c}{h+1}\\le b_h\\le1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Truncate the strip laterally by tiling lines whose outward normals are rotated by \\(\\pm\\pi/3\\) from upward vertical. Lemma 1.1 gives finiteness.</p><p>Continue a lateral exit through the removed part, strictly increasing height and outward projection by a zigzag of vertical-up and upward-outward edges until reaching the top. At fixed \\(h\\), this costs a bounded factor and has bounded multiplicity: the first hit of the cut recovers the exit. The resulting bridges go arbitrarily far laterally, so their total weight tends to zero by finiteness. Thus side-exit mass vanishes and gives (2.1).</p><p>An arch contributing to \\(A_{h+1}-A_h\\) visits the last layer. Split it at an appropriate vertex immediately below a top port into two bridges sharing that vertex. Dropping mutual avoidance gives the product \\(b_{h+1}^2\\); the shared vertex contributes \\(x_*^{-1}\\).</p><p>Using (2.1),\n\\[\n\\frac1{b_{h+1}}-\\frac1{b_h}\n\\le x_*^{-1}\\cos(\\sigma\\pi)\\frac{b_{h+1}}{b_h}\n\\le x_*^{-1}\\cos(\\sigma\\pi).\n\\]\nSummation yields the lower bound; the upper bound follows from (2.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Fixed-width cylinder convergence)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a finite limit \\(A_\\infty^{(N)}\\), and \\( \\sum_h b_h^{(N)}<\\infty. \\) For constants \\(C_N,c_N>0\\), \\( b_h^{(N)}+A_\\infty^{(N)}-A_h^{(N)} \\le C_Ne^{-c_Nh}. \\) The same estimates hold when a terminal port is …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer horizontal period \\(N&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b_h^{(N)}\\) and \\(A_h^{(N)}\\) be the cylindrical strip masses from a fixed bottom port, with terminal port summed on the top and bottom respectively.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite limit \\(A_\\infty^{(N)}\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_h b_h^{(N)}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFor constants \\(C_N,c_N&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nb_h^{(N)}+A_\\infty^{(N)}-A_h^{(N)}\n\\le C_Ne^{-c_Nh}.\n\\]\nThe same estimates hold when a terminal port is prescribed.</p>", "proof": "<p>A cylindrical bridge lifts to a plane strip bridge. Cut a plane bridge at every interior horizontal layer line crossed exactly once. Positive-height indecomposable pieces from a fixed start have total mass at most \\(1\\): if the mass restricted to heights at most \\(H\\) exceeded \\(1\\), \\(k\\)-fold concatenations would grow exponentially, whereas their mass is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\le kH}b_h\\le kH.\n\\]</p><p>The mass of pieces whose projections are simple on the cylinder is strictly below \\(1\\). Already in one layer, arbitrarily long internal zigzags yield indecomposable bridges that repeat a vertex after projection. Summing a geometric series after dropping avoidance between pieces gives \\(\\sum_hb_h^{(N)}&lt;\\infty\\). Same-boundary arcs are bounded by lifting and the planar arch bound.</p><p>Reveal configurations layer by layer, retaining occupied ports, connectivity of dangling ends, and prescribed terminals, rejecting a closed cycle when it forms. There are finitely many states and constant transition coefficients, after a lattice-period identification in the layer direction. Thus \\(b_h^{(N)}\\) and \\(A_{h+1}^{(N)}-A_h^{(N)}\\) have rational generating functions. Nonnegativity and summability exclude poles on or inside the unit circle; both sequences therefore decay exponentially. Sum the second tail. Prescribing a terminal only decreases positive sums.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The local slide and Yang–Baxter identities, transfer-row relations, and physical spectral conclusions are those in Lemmas 2.1–2.3.", "statement": "<p>Transfer-row algebra and the physical eigenline.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  q=e^{i\\lambda},\\quad t=q^2,\\quad T=t^3,\\quad\n  V=\\mathbb C\\{0,+1,-1\\},\\quad n_0=\\sqrt2-1.\n  \\]\n  Charge is the sum of spins. Transposes do not conjugate.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(C\\in V^{\\otimes2}\\) and \\(I:V\\to V^{\\otimes2}\\) by\n  \\[\n  C=|00\\rangle+\\sum_{h=\\pm1}q^{5h}|h,-h\\rangle,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  I_0=|00\\rangle+x_*\\sum_{h=\\pm1}q^{4h}|h,-h\\rangle,\n  \\qquad\n  I_h=x_*(q^h|h,0\\rangle+q^{-h}|0,h\\rangle).\n  \\]\n  Then\n  \\[\n  I^tI=n_0\\,1_V,\\quad C^tC=-n_0,\\quad C^tI=0,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  P_1=II^t/n_0,\\quad P_0=-CC^t/n_0,\\quad P_2=1-P_1-P_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The rendered checked-matrix definition is\n  \\[\n  F(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^2r_j(z)P_j,\\qquad\n  (r_0,r_1,r_2)=(b,a,ab),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{t^2-z}{t^2z-1},\\qquad b=\\frac{T-z}{Tz-1}.\n  \\]\n  It exchanges parameter slots \\(u,w\\) with \\(z=u/w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For sites \\(X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)\\) and twist \\(\\kappa\\ne0\\), define\n  \\[\n  [\\mathcal T_\\kappa(z;X)]_{\\boldsymbol l,\\boldsymbol j}\n  =\n  \\sum_{i_0,\\ldots,i_{N-1}\\in\\{0,\\pm1\\}}\n  \\kappa^{i_0}\n  \\prod_{a=1}^N\n  F(z/x_a)_{l_ai_a,i_{a-1}j_a},\n  \\qquad i_N=i_0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The local slide and Yang–Baxter identities, transfer-row relations, and physical spectral conclusions are those in Lemmas 2.1–2.3. These supply commuting rows and a simple charge-zero eigenline whose row eigenvalue is identically \\(1\\) at twist \\(\\kappa=\\pm i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies the local moves. Lemma 2.2 transports those moves through twisted rows. Lemma 2.3 identifies the physical common eigenline and its normalization. These are the two transfer outputs used later: row differentiation for boundary mass and polynomial representation for the separator pairing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 (Local slide and interchange relations)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "As rational identities, \\( F_{12}(z)F_{23}(Tz)(C\\otimes1)=1\\otimes C, \\) \\( F_{12}(z)F_{23}(t^2z)(I\\otimes1) =(1\\otimes I)F(tz), \\) and \\( F_{12}(y)F_{23}(zy)F_{12}(z) = F_{23}(z)F_{12}(zy)F_{23}(y). \\) The construction …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the tensors and checked matrices of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As rational identities,\n\\[\nF_{12}(z)F_{23}(Tz)(C\\otimes1)=1\\otimes C,\n\\]\n\\[\nF_{12}(z)F_{23}(t^2z)(I\\otimes1)\n=(1\\otimes I)F(tz),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF_{12}(y)F_{23}(zy)F_{12}(z)\n=\nF_{23}(z)F_{12}(zy)F_{23}(y).\n\\]\nThe construction also uses\n\\[\nF(1)=1,\\quad F(z)F(1/z)=1,\\quad\nF(T)=CC^t=-n_0P_0,\\quad F(t^2)=II^t=n_0P_1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Project the slides onto the three channels. Set\n\\[\nm=(1,1+1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2),\n\\qquad c=(1/n_0,\\sqrt2,2+\\sqrt2).\n\\]\nThe direct tensor contractions are\n\\[\n(1\\otimes C^t)(C\\otimes1)=1,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1\\otimes C^t)(I\\otimes1)\n=(C^t\\otimes1)(1\\otimes I)=I^t,\n\\qquad\n(1\\otimes I)C=(I\\otimes1)C,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1\\otimes I^t)(I\\otimes1)/n_0=\\sum_jm_jP_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n1\\otimes C-C\\otimes1\n=(2+\\sqrt2)(1\\otimes I-I\\otimes1)I.\n\\]\nThese follow entrywise from\n\\[\nC_{ab}=\\delta_{a,-b}q^{5a},\n\\qquad I_{ab,h}=\\delta_{h,a+b}J(a,b),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nJ(0,0)=1,\\quad J(h,-h)=x_*q^{4h},\n\\quad J(h,0)=x_*q^h,\\quad J(0,h)=x_*q^{-h},\n\\]\nand invalid labels give zero. In particular,\n\\[\n\\delta_{ah}C_{bc}-C_{ab}\\delta_{ch}\n=x_*^{-2}\\delta_{a+b+c,h}\n[J(b,c)J(a,b+c)-J(a,b)J(a+b,c)],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(1\\otimes I^t)(I\\otimes1)=x_*^2(1-CC^t)+II^t.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nP_{j,12}(1\\otimes C)=c_jP_{j,12}(1\\otimes I)I.\n\\]</p><p>Projection of the first slide reduces it to\n\\[\nr_j(z)\\!\\left[\n-\\delta_{j0}n_0r_2(Tz)\n-\\frac{(r_0-r_2)(Tz)}{n_0}\n+\\frac{(r_1-r_2)(Tz)}{n_0c_j}\n\\right]=1.\n\\]\nSandwiching the second between \\(P_{j,12}\\) and \\(P_k\\) reduces it to\n\\[\nr_j(z)\\!\\left[\n\\frac{\\delta_{j1}r_2(t^2z)}{m_k}\n-\\delta_{k1}c_j(r_0-r_2)(t^2z)\n+m_k(r_1-r_2)(t^2z)\n\\right]=r_k(tz)\n\\]\nwhenever \\(j=1\\), \\(k=1\\), or \\(j=k=2\\); in the other cases the tensor factor vanishes.</p><p>The scalar checks use \\(p=t^2\\), denominator \\((pu-1)(Tu-1)\\), and numerators\n\\[\n(p-u)(T-u),\\quad\n(p-u)(T+1)(u-1),\\quad\n(T-u)(p+1)(u-1)\n\\]\nfor \\(r_2,r_0-r_2,r_1-r_2\\). At \\(u=Tz,pz\\), the denominators become\n\\[\nT(p-z)(T-z),\\qquad(1+z)(1+tz).\n\\]\nSubstitution proves the slides. Inverse and transpose slides follow from unitarity.</p><p>For Yang–Baxter, clear denominators. Both sides have degree at most \\(4\\) in \\(z\\), and for generic \\(y\\) agree at the five distinct values\n\\[\n1,\\quad T,\\quad t^2,\\quad T/y,\\quad t^2/y.\n\\]\nAt \\(1\\) this is immediate. At \\(T,t^2\\), use the rank reductions \\(EE^t\\), \\(E=C,I\\), and slide \\(E\\) on one side and \\(E^t\\) on the other. The last two values follow by unitarity from the preceding cases at \\((zy,1/y)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (Row relations)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Rows commute in their row arguments; an adjacent-site exchange by \\(F\\) intertwines the corresponding rows.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the rows of Proposition 2 at fixed twist.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Rows commute in their row arguments; an adjacent-site exchange by \\(F\\) intertwines the corresponding rows. At generic sites, and then meromorphically,\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\kappa(x_j)\\mathcal T_\\kappa(Tx_j)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal T_\\kappa(x_j)\\mathcal T_\\kappa(t^2x_j)\n=\\mathcal T_\\kappa(tx_j).\n\\]\nInsertion of \\(C\\) at adjacent sites \\((x,Tx)\\) intertwines with the row omitting those sites. Insertion of \\(I\\) at \\((x,t^2x)\\) intertwines with the row replacing them by the site \\(tx\\). On charge zero,\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\kappa(0)=(1+\\kappa+\\kappa^{-1})1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Insert a generic invertible crossing of two auxiliary lines, move it through the sites by Yang–Baxter, and remove it in the twisted trace. Charge conservation preserves the product twist. The same argument gives site exchange.</p><p>For row fusion, take the indicated auxiliary pair in order, the second crossing each site first. The slides preserve the embedded \\(C\\) or \\(I\\) subspace, and at \\(x_j\\) a rank reduction followed by a checked identity sends the whole auxiliary space into it. The product twist on \\(C\\) is \\(1\\); on the \\(I\\)-image it is the single twist. Inverse slides give site fusion.</p><p>At row argument \\(0\\), triangularity forces the auxiliary spin to remain constant around the trace. The diagonal entries are \\(1\\) if either spin vanishes, \\(t^5\\) for equal nonzero spins, and \\(t^3\\) for opposite nonzero spins; charge zero gives the displayed scalar.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Physical eigenline)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Powers of the row converge exponentially to a rank-one projection with empty-to-empty entry \\(1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take homogeneous sites \\(X=(1,\\ldots,1)\\), \\(\\kappa=\\pm i\\), and row argument \\(z=t\\) or the reflected honeycomb argument \\(z=t^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The empty tensor has every spin zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Powers of the row converge exponentially to a rank-one projection with empty-to-empty entry \\(1\\). Thus eigenvalue \\(1\\) is simple and every other eigenvalue has modulus less than \\(1\\).</p><p>Near the physical data there is a common analytic charge-zero eigenline. Its eigenvalue is rational in the row argument and analytic in the other data near regular arguments. At \\(\\kappa=\\pm i\\), this eigenvalue is identically \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For geometric rhombi, put \\(v=3\\theta/8\\), \\(z=e^{2iv}\\), and\n\\[\nD=\\sin(2\\lambda+v)\\sin(3\\lambda+v).\n\\]\nThe unoriented local weights are\n\\[\nu_1=\\frac{\\sin(2\\lambda)\\sin(3\\lambda-v)}D,\\quad\nu_2=\\frac{\\sin(2\\lambda)\\sin v}D,\\quad\no=\\frac{\\sin(3\\lambda-v)\\sin v}D,\n\\]\n\\[\nw_1=\\frac{\\sin(3\\lambda-v)\\sin(2\\lambda-v)}D,\\qquad\nw_2=\\frac{\\sin(v-\\lambda)\\sin v}D.\n\\]\nOrient arcs with phase \\(e^{iW/4}\\), and multiply by gauge\n\\[\ne^{5iv(i-k)/3}=e^{5iv(l-j)/3}.\n\\]\nThe resulting local matrix is the checked matrix. At \\(v=\\lambda\\),\n\\[\nu_1=x_*,\\qquad u_2=o=w_1=x_*^2,\\qquad w_2=0,\n\\]\ngiving two honeycomb triangles; reflection gives \\(v=2\\lambda\\).</p><p>At homogeneous physical data, contractible loops have turns \\(\\pm2\\pi\\) and cancel after orientation summation. Essential simple loops have winding \\(\\pm1\\), total turn \\(0\\), and twists \\(\\kappa^{\\pm1}\\), so also cancel. The total-turn assertion follows from the exterior-angle sum in the annulus between the loop and a distant parallel geodesic. Gauge factors telescope.</p><p>A surviving crossing arc has exponentially small mass by Lemma 1.3. Without crossing arcs, upper and lower boundary systems factor into half-cylinder amplitudes; a failure of factorization must reach halfway through the height. There are finitely many boundary pairings and orientations, and dropping avoidance bounds them by products of arc masses. This gives the exponential rank-one limit. Its empty-to-empty entry is \\(1\\), since nonempty closed systems cancel at finite height.</p><p>Finite-dimensional perturbation gives a simple analytic eigenline; commutation makes it common, and charge conservation keeps it in charge zero. Its row eigenvalue is rational.</p><p>For nearby real spectral angles \\(z=e^{2iu}\\), \\(x_j=e^{2i\\alpha_j}\\), embed rows with side directions \\(8u/3\\) and bottom directions \\(8\\alpha_j/3\\). The repeated zigzag boundary projects monotonically perpendicular to the row direction, so rows do not overlap. Loops still cancel. Gauge factors telescope because \\(v=u-\\alpha_j\\) and \\(i-k=l-j_{\\rm spin}\\). Every empty-to-empty power is therefore \\(1\\). Analytic continuation and strict spectral dominance force the distinguished eigenvalue and projection entry to equal \\(1\\); rational continuation finishes the argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.4 (Polynomial vacuum)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a charge-zero vector polynomial \\(\\Psi_N(X)\\), homogeneous of degree \\(\\binom N2\\), with degree at most \\(N-1\\) in each variable, satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(V,F,C,I,q,t,T\\) from Proposition 2 and twist \\(\\zeta=t^2=i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\rho X=(x_N,x_1,\\ldots,x_{N-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Pi_\\kappa\\) rotate spin slots in the same way, multiplying by \\(\\kappa^{h_N}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s_i\\) exchange adjacent site parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">For a remaining list \\(Y\\), put\n  \\[\n  s_C(a;Y)=(1+T)a\\prod_{x\\in Y}(x-t^5a)(x-t^6a).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a charge-zero vector polynomial \\(\\Psi_N(X)\\), homogeneous of degree \\(\\binom N2\\), with degree at most \\(N-1\\) in each variable, satisfying\n\\[\nF_{i,i+1}(x_i/x_{i+1})\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(s_iX),\n\\qquad\n\\Pi_\\zeta\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(\\rho X).\n\\]\nFor component \\(\\boldsymbol h\\),\n\\[\nh_j=+1\\Longrightarrow \\deg_{x_j}\\Psi_{N,\\boldsymbol h}\\le N-2,\n\\qquad\nh_j=-1\\Longrightarrow x_j\\mid\\Psi_{N,\\boldsymbol h}.\n\\]\nIt has the cup reduction\n\\[\n\\Psi_N(\\ldots,a,Ta,\\ldots)\n=s_C(a;Y)\\,\\iota_C\\Psi_{N-2}(Y),\n\\]\nand the last-spin-zero projection at \\(x_N=0\\) is\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_{j&lt;N}x_j\\right)\\Psi_{N-1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(N\\). Lemma 3.1 gives the bases; Lemmas 3.2–3.3 give polynomiality, bounds, exchanges, and cup reductions.</p><p>For cyclicity, lower-size cyclicity and cups imply\n\\[\n\\Pi_\\zeta^{-1}\\Psi(\\rho X)-\\Psi(X)\n=\n\\prod_{i&lt;k\\le N-1}(x_k-Tx_i)\\,E(X).\n\\]\nThe quotient is multiaffine in its first \\(N-1\\) variables. The refined bounds give exactly the bit-dependent spin support in Lemma 3.4. Exchanges among those variables give its exchange equation. Since \\(N-1\\ge3\\), Lemma 3.4 kills the cyclic discrepancy. This completes the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At sizes two and three, \\( S_2(a,b)=a+b,\\qquad S_3(a,b,c)=\\sum_{i\\ne j}x_i^2x_j+2\\sqrt2\\,abc. \\) For first pair \\((h,-h)\\) and last spin zero, \\( f_h(a,b;c)=2\\sin\\lambda\\,t^hL_h(a,b)P(a,b;c), \\) where …", "statement": "<p>Initial vectors for Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Psi_0=1\\), \\(\\Psi_{1,0}=1\\), with other size-one components zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_N\\) denote the empty component.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At sizes two and three,\n\\[\nS_2(a,b)=a+b,\\qquad\nS_3(a,b,c)=\\sum_{i\\ne j}x_i^2x_j+2\\sqrt2\\,abc.\n\\]\nFor first pair \\((h,-h)\\) and last spin zero,\n\\[\nf_h(a,b;c)=2\\sin\\lambda\\,t^hL_h(a,b)P(a,b;c),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nL_+=b,\\quad L_-=a,\\quad\nP(a,b;c)=(c+ta)(c+b/t).\n\\]\nAt size two omit \\(P\\); cyclicity specifies the remaining components. These vectors satisfy Proposition 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cup, zero-node, and degree conditions follow by substitution. For the first exchange, the explicit checked blocks reduce to\n\\[\nP(b,a;c)=w_1P(a,b;c)+\\frac{u_2S}{2\\sin\\lambda\\,ab},\n\\]\n\\[\nP(c,b;a)=u_1\\sqrt{a/b}\\,P(c,a;b)+oP(b,c;a),\n\\]\n\\[\nP(a,c;b)=oP(c,a;b)+u_1\\sqrt{b/a}\\,P(b,c;a),\n\\]\nwith the square-root branch corresponding to \\(e^{2iv}=a/b\\). At size two, only the first identity is needed, with both \\(P\\)'s replaced by \\(1\\).</p><p>Use\n\\[\n1-w_1=u_2\\cos v/\\sin\\lambda,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos(2\\lambda-v)-w_1\\cos(2\\lambda+v)\n=\\frac{u_2}{2\\sin\\lambda}(\\cos2v+\\sqrt2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nu_1e^{iv}+o/t=u_1e^{-iv}+ot\n=\\frac{\\sin(3\\lambda-v)}{\\sin(3\\lambda+v)}\n=\\frac{b+ta}{a+tb}.\n\\]\nThe \\(w_2\\)-terms on occupied pairs cancel because \\(t^2=i\\), as do the two contributions to the empty-component change. Rotation gives the remaining exchanges.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Interpolate \\(V_i\\) with multiplier \\(L_i\\) in last spin \\(+\\), and \\(x_NL_i/(Tx_i)\\) otherwise; in last spin zero add \\( \\prod_{d<N}(x_d-x_N/T)\\Psi_{N-1}. \\) The resulting vector is polynomial, has all degree and …", "statement": "<p>Cup interpolation, spectator bounds, and propagation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(N\\ge4\\) and assume Proposition 3 at all smaller sizes.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(Y_i=(x_1,\\ldots,\\widehat{x_i},\\ldots,x_{N-1})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(x_N=Tx_i\\), prescribe\n  \\[\n  V_i=s_C(x_i;Y_i)\n  F_{N-1,N}(Tx_i/x_{N-1})\\cdots\n  F_{i+1,i+2}(Tx_i/x_{i+1})\\,\n  \\iota_{C,i}\\Psi_{N-2}(Y_i).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  L_i(X)=\\prod_{\\substack{d&lt;N\\\\d\\ne i}}\n         \\frac{x_N-Tx_d}{T(x_i-x_d)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Interpolate \\(V_i\\) with multiplier \\(L_i\\) in last spin \\(+\\), and \\(x_NL_i/(Tx_i)\\) otherwise; in last spin zero add\n\\[\n\\prod_{d&lt;N}(x_d-x_N/T)\\Psi_{N-1}.\n\\]\nThe resulting vector is polynomial, has all degree and refined spin bounds in Proposition 3, satisfies exchanges among the first \\(N-1\\) sites, and has every cup specialization \\(x_k=Tx_i\\), \\(i&lt;k\\), with the prescribed transport.</p>", "proof": "<p>At each crossing with \\(x_d\\), the denominator factors are proportional to\n\\[\n(x_d-t^5x_i)(x_d-t^6x_i),\n\\]\nalready present in \\(s_C\\). Thus \\(V_i\\) is polynomial.</p><p>The prescriptions are exchange-covariant. Away from the mark use lower-size exchange and Yang–Baxter; at the mark use\n\\[\nF_{12}(a/b)F_{23}(Ta/b)(C\\otimes1)=1\\otimes C\n\\]\nand its inverse. Apparent poles at coincident nodes cancel because the prescriptions agree there, by exchange covariance and unitarity. The remaining simple poles at \\(x_i=0\\) cancel because \\(x_i\\mid V_i\\). Homogeneity gives total degree \\(\\binom N2\\). Interpolation proves exchanges among the first \\(N-1\\) sites.</p><p>For a spectator \\(x_j\\), \\(i\\ne j\\), the lower-size bound and two spectator factors give degree at most \\(N-1\\). At \\(x_j\\to\\infty\\), the crossing tends to \\(F(0)\\), whose leading output site spin cannot exceed its input spin; the plus-spin degree loss is retained. At \\(x_j=0\\), the opposite triangularity retains the minus-spin zero. The interpolation multipliers are bounded at both ends.</p><p>For \\(V_j\\),\n\\[\nV_j=O(x_j^{2N-3})\\quad(x_j\\to\\infty),\\qquad V_j=O(x_j)\\quad(x_j\\to0),\n\\]\nwhereas \\(L_j\\) has orders \\(x_j^{-(N-2)}\\), \\(1\\), and \\(x_NL_j/(Tx_j)\\) has orders \\(x_j^{-(N-1)}\\), \\(x_j^{-1}\\). These give the unrefined bounds. If both site \\(j\\) and the final site are plus at infinity, the carried cup spin starts minus and cannot increase under leading \\(F(\\infty)\\), killing the leading coefficient. At zero, site \\(j\\) minus and final spin not plus force a carried plus spin that cannot decrease under \\(F(0)\\), again killing the leading term.</p><p>To propagate an adjacent cup among the first \\(N-1\\) sites, check last-variable interpolation nodes. Disjoint cups commute, including scalars, because\n\\[\n\\prod_{y\\in\\{d,Td\\}}(y-t^5a)(y-t^6a)\n=T^2\\prod_{r\\in\\{t^2,T,t^5,t^6\\}}(d-ra)\n\\]\nis unchanged by interchanging \\(a,d\\). At the overlapping node \\(x_N=Tx_{j+1}\\), both sides vanish. At \\(x_N=Tx_j=x_{j+1}\\), the first crossing is the checked identity and leaves the required cup. The zero-node scalars agree because \\(t^{5+6}=T\\). Interpolation and the established exchanges give all transported cup reductions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The remaining exchange between sites \\(N-1,N\\) holds.", "statement": "<p>The last exchange.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the inductive construction of Lemma 3.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The remaining exchange between sites \\(N-1,N\\) holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Clear denominators in the discrepancy:\n\\[\nD=(t^2x_{N-1}-x_N)(Tx_{N-1}-x_N)\n\\left[F_{N-1,N}(x_{N-1}/x_N)\\Psi(X)-\\Psi(s_{N-1}X)\\right].\n\\]\nCup reduction and lower-size exchange show that \\(D\\) vanishes on every \\(x_k=Tx_i\\), \\(i&lt;k\\), except possibly \\((i,k)=(N-1,N)\\). Hence\n\\[\nD=QE,\\qquad\nQ=\\prod_{\\substack{i&lt;k\\\\(i,k)\\ne(N-1,N)}}(x_k-Tx_i).\n\\]\nEvery spectator \\(x_j\\), \\(j\\le N-2\\), occurs in \\(N-1\\) factors of \\(Q\\), exhausting its degree bound. Thus \\(E\\) is spectator-independent. The refined bounds force every spectator spin to be zero.</p><p>For adjacent spectators,\n\\[\nQ(s_jX)/Q(X)=b(x_j/x_{j+1}),\n\\]\nso their exchange forces \\(FE=bE\\), putting their pair in channel \\(1\\). A nonzero pure \\(|00\\rangle\\) is not in \\(\\operatorname{im}I\\); there are at least two spectators. Thus \\(E=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Claim 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(E=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) be tensor-valued and multiaffine in at least three consecutive variables.</p><p class=\"li\">In the coefficient of a bit string, bit \\(0\\) permits spins \\(0,+\\), and bit \\(1\\) permits spins \\(0,-\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Adjacent exchange satisfies\n  \\[\n  E(s_jX)=\n  (aP_2+P_1+(a/b)P_0)_{j,j+1}E(X),\n  \\]\n  where \\(a=a(x_j/x_{j+1})\\), \\(b=b(x_j/x_{j+1})\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(E=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The two uncanceled denominator factors of \\(a/b\\),\n\\[\nt^2x_j-x_{j+1},\\qquad Tx_{j+1}-x_j,\n\\]\nmust divide \\(P_0E\\). Their product has degree two in each variable, whereas \\(P_0E\\) is multiaffine. Hence \\(P_0E=0\\).</p><p>The channel-\\(1\\) part is symmetric. The channel-\\(2\\) part is divisible by \\(x_{j+1}-t^2x_j\\), with quotient independent of this pair. Thus\n\\[\nE_{10}=(P_1-t^2P_2)E_{01},\n\\]\nan invertible operation on channels \\(1,2\\). It suffices to kill sorted bit strings.</p><p>Equal adjacent bits require channel \\(1\\); the allowed support makes the pair proportional to \\(I_+\\) for \\(00\\), or \\(I_-\\) for \\(11\\). Three equal bits are impossible because they produce a forbidden neighboring pair \\(00\\) or \\(hh\\). For \\(0011\\), the tensor factors as \\(I_+\\otimes I_-\\), whose middle cup contraction is nonzero for end spins \\(+,-\\), contrary to \\(P_0E=0\\). For \\(001\\), fixing the first spin plus and then zero excludes third spin zero and then minus. The argument for \\(011\\) is reversed. These exhaust sorted strings of length at least three.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\Omega\\) is the normalized physical lower half-cylinder state, with empty component \\(1\\), and \\( \\mathcal T_\\zeta(z;X)\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(X). \\) The reciprocal transpose \\(\\Omega(X^{-1})^t\\) is the normalized left state …", "statement": "<p>Physical normalization, empty component, and normalized reductions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Psi_N\\) be from Proposition 3 and \\(S_N\\) its empty component.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\Omega(X)=\\Psi_N(X)/S_N(X)\\) generically.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  D(a,b)=a^2+\\sqrt2\\,ab+b^2,\\qquad\n  K(a,b)=\\frac{a^2-b^2}{D(a,b)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\Omega\\) is the normalized physical lower half-cylinder state, with empty component \\(1\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\zeta(z;X)\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(X).\n\\]\nThe reciprocal transpose \\(\\Omega(X^{-1})^t\\) is the normalized left state at twist \\(\\zeta^{-1}\\). The symmetric polynomial \\(S_N\\) and normalized reductions are specified in Lemmas 4.2–4.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 supplies the geometric normalization and symmetry needed for Lemma 4.2. Lemma 4.2 determines the denominator, and Lemma 4.3 gives the normalized interpolation reductions.</p><p>The scalar convention also agrees with the reversed numerator/Vandermonde convention in the paper's Remark 4.2:\n\\[\nS_{2n}(X)=\n\\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{D(x_i,x_j)}{x_j-x_i}\n\\operatorname{Pf}\\!\\left[\n\\frac{x_j^2-x_i^2}{D(x_i,x_j)}\n\\right].\n\\]\nThe two sign changes multiply to\n\\((-1)^{n(2n-1)}(-1)^n=1\\). Rescaling lattice coordinates by \\(\\sqrt3\\) preserves vertices, weights, and separation of marks, hence preserves the physical \\(F_n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal T_\\zeta(z;X)\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(X), \\) and \\(\\Psi_N(X^{-1})^t\\) is a left eigenvector at twist \\(\\zeta^{-1}\\).", "statement": "<p>Eigenline identification and symmetry of the empty component.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 4 at generic sites near homogeneity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\zeta(z;X)\\Psi_N(X)=\\Psi_N(X),\n\\]\nand \\(\\Psi_N(X^{-1})^t\\) is a left eigenvector at twist \\(\\zeta^{-1}\\). The normalized vectors agree with the physical states from Proposition 2, and \\(S_N\\) is symmetric in all sites.</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(z=x_N\\), the last crossing is the checked identity. The row is rotation followed by transport of the first parameter to the end, so exchange and cyclicity prove the equality there. Exchange gives all \\(x_j\\); row fusion/inversion gives all \\(Tx_j\\); triangularity gives \\(0\\). Clearing denominators leaves degree at most \\(2N\\), so the \\(2N+1\\) generic points suffice.</p><p>The left identity follows similarly: reverse transport through symmetric crossings exchanges reciprocal parameters, and the final rotation cancels because\n\\[\n\\Pi_{\\zeta^{-1}}^t\\Pi_\\zeta=1.\n\\]\nThe empty component is not identically zero by cup reduction. Proposition 2 therefore identifies \\(\\Omega\\) with the normalized physical state.</p><p>For symmetry, take nearby real angles with \\(\\alpha_i&gt;\\alpha_{i+1}\\). Add the rhombus above the two corresponding upper-boundary steps, exchanging their order without crossing the twist seam. With empty final output, gauges telescope and loops cancel even at finite height. Thus the added crossing maps one normalized half-cylinder state to the other without scalar factor. Comparing with polynomial exchange gives \\(S_N(s_iX)=S_N(X)\\), first on an open real-angle set and then polynomially.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.6 (Empty component)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( S_N(X)= \\prod_{i<j}\\frac{D(x_i,x_j)}{x_i-x_j} \\operatorname{Pf}[K(x_i,x_j)]. \\) It satisfies \\( S_N(X^{-1})=\\frac{S_N(X)}{\\prod_i x_i^{N-1}}, \\) \\( S_N(a,t^2a,Y) = \\sqrt2\\,ta\\prod_{y\\in Y}(y-t^5a)\\, S_{N-1}(ta,Y), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(D,K,S_N\\) from Proposition 4. For odd \\(N\\), append a final Pfaffian index with \\(K_{i,N+1}=1\\), corresponding to parameter zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nS_N(X)=\n\\prod_{i&lt;j}\\frac{D(x_i,x_j)}{x_i-x_j}\n\\operatorname{Pf}[K(x_i,x_j)].\n\\]\nIt satisfies\n\\[\nS_N(X^{-1})=\\frac{S_N(X)}{\\prod_i x_i^{N-1}},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_N(a,t^2a,Y)\n=\n\\sqrt2\\,ta\\prod_{y\\in Y}(y-t^5a)\\,\nS_{N-1}(ta,Y),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n[x_N^{N-1}]S_N(X)=S_{N-1}(x_1,\\ldots,x_{N-1}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The cleared numerator is alternating, so Vandermonde denominators cancel; the factors \\(D\\) cancel the other possible poles. Boundedness of \\(K\\) at infinity gives the one-site degree bound.</p><p>At \\((a,Ta)\\), only that pair's own Pfaffian matching survives, yielding \\(s_CS_{N-2}\\). At a last zero parameter, the zero-node prescription follows; with a dummy zero only their mutual pairing remains. Symmetry and interpolation identify this polynomial with \\(S_N\\). Inversion gives reciprocity, and reciprocity with the zero-node identity gives the leading coefficient.</p><p>For the second fusion, compare both sides in a spectator variable. Cup reductions are compatible because\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(a-t^5d)(a-t^6d)(t^2a-t^5d)(t^2a-t^6d)\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=(d-t^5a)(Td-t^5a)(ta-t^5d)(ta-t^6d).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach of the other \\(N-3\\) spectators gives roots at ratios \\(T,T^{-1}\\); the zero-node gives one more. Leading coefficients agree, leaving degree at most \\(N-2\\). The \\(2(N-3)+1\\) roots suffice for \\(N\\ge4\\); sizes two and three follow from Lemma 3.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Corollary 3.7 (Normalized reductions)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At adjacent \\((a,Ta)\\), \\(\\Omega\\) reduces by insertion of \\(C\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the generic normalized states \\(\\Omega\\) from Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At adjacent \\((a,Ta)\\), \\(\\Omega\\) reduces by insertion of \\(C\\). At adjacent \\((a,t^2a)\\), it reduces by insertion of \\(I\\), replacing the two sites by \\(ta\\). When a parameter tends to \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), projection onto spin zero at that slot tends to the reduced \\(\\Omega\\), with scalar factor \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cup scalars for \\(\\Psi\\) and \\(S_N\\) agree and cancel.</p><p>At \\((a,t^2a)\\), the exchange pole forces channel \\(1\\). Row intertwining reduces the eigenvalue-\\(1\\) problem to the smaller row. Its line is generically unique: it is unique at homogeneous sites by Proposition 2, and nonvanishing rational minors preserve this generically. Empty-component normalization fixes the scalar.</p><p>At zero, use rotation and the zero-node prescription. At infinity, the degree bounds give a generic limit with no plus spin in the extreme slot; triangularity after spin-zero projection gives the reduced row. Uniqueness and the nonzero leading coefficient of \\(S_N\\) fix the scalar to \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For homogeneous balanced blocks, \\( X_{n,n}(1,\\ldots,1;\\kappa)=F_n(\\eta). \\) The generic rational family \\(X_{n,m}\\) is uniquely characterized by Lemma 5.2.", "statement": "<p>Separator contraction and its interpolation characterization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Split the \\(N=n+m\\) ordered sites into blocks \\(A,B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\kappa\\ne0\\), put \\(\\eta=\\kappa+\\kappa^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D^A_{\\kappa/\\zeta}\\) multiply a spin tensor by\n  \\((\\kappa/\\zeta)^{\\sum_{j\\in A}h_j}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X_{n,m}(X;\\kappa)\n  =\\Omega(X^{-1})^tD^A_{\\kappa/\\zeta}\\Omega(X).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For homogeneous balanced blocks,\n\\[\nX_{n,n}(1,\\ldots,1;\\kappa)=F_n(\\eta).\n\\]\nThe generic rational family \\(X_{n,m}\\) is uniquely characterized by Lemma 5.2. Its homogeneous balanced specialization is a polynomial in \\(\\eta\\) with nonnegative coefficients and constant term \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 gives the physical positive interpretation and normalization. Lemma 5.2 gives the algebraic characterization later used to identify the finite color formula. Neither assertion requires nonvanishing of \\(S_N\\) at every complex specialization; physical singular formulas are evaluated through the analytic states of Proposition 2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Proposition 4.1 (Physical separator pairing)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( X_{n,n}(1,\\ldots,1;\\kappa)=F_n(\\kappa+\\kappa^{-1}). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the balanced homogeneous contraction from Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nX_{n,n}(1,\\ldots,1;\\kappa)=F_n(\\kappa+\\kappa^{-1}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For finite height \\(H\\), use\n\\[\n|u_H\\rangle=\\mathcal T_\\zeta(t)^H|0\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\langle v_H|=\\langle0|\\mathcal T_{\\zeta^{-1}}(t)^H.\n\\]\nEmpty-to-empty entries are \\(1\\), and Proposition 2 gives convergence to the normalized lower and upper states.</p><p>Gluing in\n\\(\\langle v_H|D^A_{\\kappa/\\zeta}|u_H\\rangle\\)\nmakes disjoint polygons. Gauges telescope. There are directed seams from the lower boundary to \\(f\\), from \\(f\\) to the upper boundary, and from \\(f'\\) to \\(f\\), carrying \\(\\zeta,\\zeta^{-1},\\kappa/\\zeta\\).</p><p>For an oriented simple polygon, let \\(L(a)\\) indicate that \\(a\\) lies on its left. A seam \\(a\\to b\\) has signed crossing number \\(L(a)-L(b)\\). Including turn phases, both contractible polygons oriented interior-left and essential polygons oriented upper-end-left have total factor\n\\[\n\\zeta^{1-L(f)-L(f')}\\kappa^{L(f')-L(f)}.\n\\]\nReversal gives its inverse. Equal indicators give \\(\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}=0\\); unequal indicators give \\(\\eta\\). Exactly separating polygons remain.</p><p>At finite height this is a positive-coefficient polynomial of degree at most \\(n\\), since each separating polygon uses a distinct block-\\(A\\) port. Coefficients increase with height. Convergence at any positive \\(\\eta\\) bounds each coefficient, and the limits are precisely the infinite-cylinder polygon sums. Pass coefficientwise to the limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Proposition 4.3 (Interpolation of the pairing)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The family \\(X_{n,m}\\) is uniquely determined among rational families by:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use generic sites and \\(\\kappa,\\eta\\) from Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(X_{n,m}\\) is uniquely determined among rational families by:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Separate symmetry in \\(A,B\\), and polynomiality of \\(S_N^2X_{n,m}\\), of degree at most \\(2(N-1)\\) per site.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Within \\(A\\),\n   \\[\n   X_{n,m}(a,Ta,A';B)=X_{n-2,m}(A';B),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   X_{n,m}(a,t^2a,A';B)=X_{n-1,m}(ta,A';B),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{a\\to0,\\infty}X_{n,m}(a,A';B)\n   =X_{n-1,m}(A';B),\n   \\]\n   with the analogous \\(B\\)-rules.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(X_{0,0}=1\\), and for balanced positive size,\n   \\[\n   X_{n,n}(a_1,\\ldots,a_n;Ta_n,\\ldots,Ta_1)\n   =(1+\\eta)^n.\n   \\]\nPair reductions require a block of size at least \\(2\\), and extreme reductions a block of size at least \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Exchange, inverse exchange, transpose symmetry, and charge conservation give separate symmetry. Reciprocity of \\(S_N\\) converts \\(S_N^2X_{n,m}\\) into a contraction of polynomials, giving the degree bound.</p><p>At extreme parameters, complementary spin vanishing leaves only the spin-zero limit. At a cup, the right state inserts \\(C\\); the reciprocal state inserts \\(C/r_0(T)\\), and\n\\[\nC^tC=r_0(T)=-n_0\n\\]\ncancels the scalar. The block twist preserves the cup. The \\(I\\)-fusion is identical using \\(F(t^2)=n_0P_1\\), \\(I^tI=n_0\\,1\\), and the single-site twist.</p><p>At nested balanced cups, the right amplitudes are \\(\\prod q^{5h}\\), with opposite matched spins in \\(B\\). Rotating \\(B\\) in the reciprocal state gives \\(\\prod\\zeta^hq^{-5h}\\). Each matched pair therefore contributes\n\\(\\sum_{h=0,\\pm1}\\kappa^h=1+\\eta\\).</p><p>For uniqueness, multiply a difference at \\((n,m)\\) by \\(S_N^2\\), calling it \\(A(X)\\), and assume smaller-size agreement. Extreme reductions give \\(x_i\\mid A\\) and \\(\\deg_{x_i}A\\le2N-3\\). The two pair reductions in both orders give the divisor\n\\[\nQ(X)=\n\\left(\\prod_i x_i\\right)\n\\prod_{P\\in\\{A,B\\}}\n\\prod_{\\substack{i&lt;j\\\\i,j\\in P}}\n\\prod_{d\\in\\{2,3,5,6\\}}(x_j-t^dx_i).\n\\]\nIn an \\(A\\)-site,\n\\[\n\\deg_{x_i}(A/Q)\\le2(m-n),\n\\]\nand in a \\(B\\)-site it is at most \\(2(n-m)\\). Unbalanced differences vanish. In the balanced case the quotient is scalar, killed by the nested-cup specialization, generically off the zeros of \\(Q,S_N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1 (Boundary exponent)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( b_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)}. \\) The half-plane arch mass from a fixed bottom port to the right is \\( \\frac1{2\\cos(3\\pi/8)}. \\) Its height and diameter tails both have exponent \\(-1/4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the critical strip bridge masses of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nb_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe half-plane arch mass from a fixed bottom port to the right is\n\\[\n\\frac1{2\\cos(3\\pi/8)}.\n\\]\nIts height and diameter tails both have exponent \\(-1/4\\). This result uses the row relations and physical eigenline, independently of the polynomial vacuum and separator asymptotics.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.1–6.5 compute the cylinder-exclusion exponent by a row derivative and positive polynomial kernels. Lemma 6.6 transfers it to strip height. Finally,\n\\[\nb_h=1-\\cos(3\\lambda)A_h\n\\]\nis the missing half-plane arch mass multiplied by a fixed constant, giving the asserted bridge exponent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(R\\in\\mathcal G_m\\) satisfying \\( \\int[-R(s)]f(s)\\,d\\mu(s)=f(0) \\qquad(f\\in\\mathcal F_m), \\) and \\( [z]H=2\\sin\\lambda\\,e^{-3i\\lambda}R(-1), \\) \\( \\frac1{2\\cos3\\lambda}-G_N =\\frac{\\sin\\lambda}{N}[-R(-1)]. \\)", "statement": "<p>Row susceptibility and excluded arch mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N=2m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_N\\) be the half-plane mass of rightward arches with displacement \\(0&lt;d&lt;N\\) whose projections onto the period-\\(N\\) cylinder are simple.</p><p class=\"li\">Differentiate the common row eigenvalue near \\(\\kappa=i\\) with respect to \\(\\eta=\\kappa+\\kappa^{-1}\\) at \\(\\eta=0\\). In homogeneous data denote it by \\(H(\\theta)\\), where \\(z=e^{2iv}\\), \\(\\theta=2v-3\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(s=p^2\\), \\(x=\\lambda p\\), and let \\(\\mu\\) be the pushforward to \\(s\\ge0\\) of\n  \\[\n  \\frac{\\cosh3x\\,\\sinh x}{\\sinh8x}\\,dp.\n  \\]\n  Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_m=\\operatorname{span}\\{s^j,s^jp\\tanh x:0\\le j&lt;m\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal G_m=\\operatorname{span}\\{s^j,s^jp\\coth x:0\\le j&lt;m\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(R\\in\\mathcal G_m\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\int[-R(s)]f(s)\\,d\\mu(s)=f(0)\n\\qquad(f\\in\\mathcal F_m), \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n[z]H=2\\sin\\lambda\\,e^{-3i\\lambda}R(-1), \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac1{2\\cos3\\lambda}-G_N\n=\\frac{\\sin\\lambda}{N}[-R(-1)]. \\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Triangularity gives \\(H\\to1\\) at \\(z=0,\\infty\\). Reflection \\(v\\leftrightarrow3\\lambda-v\\), first geometrically near the honeycomb arguments and then analytically, makes \\(H\\) even in \\(\\theta\\). Its denominator may be \\((\\cos\\lambda+\\cos\\theta)^N\\).</p><p>Differentiated row fusion gives, at each site,\n\\[\nH(z)+H(Tz)=0,\\qquad\nH(z)+H(t^2z)-H(tz)=0.\n\\]\nCoalescing distinct sites to \\(1\\) gives zero jets through order \\(N-1\\). Evenness yields\n\\[\nH^{(2j)}(3\\lambda)=0,\\qquad\nH^{(2j+1)}(3\\lambda)=2H^{(2j+1)}(\\lambda)\n\\quad(0\\le j&lt;m).\n\\]</p><p>Represent\n\\[\nH(\\theta)=1+\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{\\theta p}\n\\frac{\\sinh(\\lambda p)U(p^2)+p\\cosh(\\lambda p)V(p^2)}\n     {\\sinh\\pi p}\\,dp,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\deg U,\\deg V&lt;m\\). The basis follows from\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{e^{\\theta p}\\sinh(\\alpha p)}{\\sinh\\pi p}\\,dp\n=\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\cos\\theta+\\cos\\alpha}\n\\]\nand successive even \\(\\theta\\)-derivatives and \\(\\alpha\\)-derivatives. The transform follows by contour shifting the principal-value integral\n\\(\\operatorname{pv}\\int e^{up}/\\sinh\\pi p\\,dp=\\tan(u/2)\\).</p><p>Set \\(R=U+p\\coth x\\,V\\). The jet conditions become (5.1), using\n\\[\n\\cosh3x=(2\\cosh2x-1)\\cosh x,\n\\quad\n\\sinh3x-2\\sinh x=(2\\cosh2x-1)\\sinh x.\n\\]\nShift the \\(p\\)-contour just above \\(i\\) to extract (5.2).</p><p>For the path interpretation, add one row of parameter \\(v\\) above a finite homogeneous stack and divide by the stack's empty-to-empty value. Spectral convergence gives the row eigenvalue, uniformly in nearby twist and regular extra-row arguments. Its derivative counts one essential loop using the extra row. With empty top, it is either horizontal, of weight \\(o^N\\), or contains equal positive numbers of \\(u_1,u_2\\). Since\n\\[\no=\\frac{(1-z)(1-t^{-3}z)}{(1-t^2z)(1-Tz)},\n\\qquad\nu_1u_2\\sim-2e^{5i\\lambda}z,\n\\]\nonly a single lower arch contributes besides the horizontal loop to \\([z]\\). Its displacement is less than \\(N\\), and exactly one of its two horizontal closures is essential. Thus\n\\[\n[z]H=N([z]o-2e^{5i\\lambda}G_N),\n\\qquad\n[z]o=\\frac{2e^{5i\\lambda}}{2\\cos3\\lambda}.\n\\]\nCombine with (5.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2 (Positive pole representation)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The weights \\(v_Av_BZ_N(w)\\) have finite positive sum and define a probability law after normalization.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The poles of \\(p\\tanh(\\lambda p)\\) have \\(a=(8l)^2\\), \\(l=1/2,3/2,\\ldots\\); those of \\(p\\coth(\\lambda p)\\) have \\(b=(8l)^2\\), \\(l=1,2,\\ldots\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For subsets \\(A,B\\) of size \\(m\\) of the respective pole sets, put\n  \\[\n  \\pi_A(s)=\\prod_{a\\in A}(1+s/a),\\quad\n  \\pi_B(s)=\\prod_{b\\in B}(1+s/b),\\quad\n  dw=\\frac{d\\mu}{\\pi_A\\pi_B}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_d^w(u,v)=\\sum_{j&lt;d}P_j(u)P_j(v)\\), where \\(P_j\\) are orthonormal polynomials for \\(w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  v_A=\\Delta(A)^2\\prod_{a\\in A}a^{1-N},\n  \\qquad\n  Z_N(w)=\\frac1{N!}\\int\\Delta(s_1,\\ldots,s_N)^2\\prod_i dw(s_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The weights \\(v_Av_BZ_N(w)\\) have finite positive sum and define a probability law after normalization. Under it,\n\\[\n-R(-1)=\n\\mathbb E_{A,B}\n\\frac{K_N^w(0,-1)}{\\pi_A(0)\\pi_B(-1)}. \\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nEvery summand is positive, and\n\\[\n\\pi_A(0)=1,\\qquad\n\\sin\\lambda/\\lambda\\le\\pi_B(-1)\\le1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use\n\\[\np\\tanh x=\\frac2\\lambda s\\sum_a\\frac1{s+a},\n\\qquad\np\\coth x=\\frac1\\lambda+\\frac2\\lambda s\\sum_b\\frac1{s+b}.\n\\]\nTheir full products are \\(\\cosh x\\) and \\(\\sinh x/x\\). These follow by residues of \\(f(w)/(w^2-x^2)\\), \\(f=\\tanh,\\coth\\), and integrating logarithmic derivatives.</p><p>Expanding the last \\(m\\) rows of a test-basis determinant by these fractions and antisymmetrizing poles gives, up to a common nonzero signed constant,\n\\[\n\\Delta(s_1,\\ldots,s_N)\n\\sum_A\\frac{v_A}{\\prod_i\\pi_A(s_i)}.\n\\]\nThe two pole Vandermondes arise from powers of poles and a Cauchy–Vandermonde determinant. The trial determinant has the \\(B\\)-version. Truncate pole sums first; after removing common sign and node Vandermonde, the sums increase positively for nodes above \\(-16\\). Exponential tails give convergence of the determinant integrals.</p><p>For column bases \\(f,g\\), let \\(M=\\int fg^t\\,d\\mu\\). Andréief's identity gives\n\\(\\det M\\) as the integral of the two evaluation determinants divided by \\(N!\\), hence a common nonzero constant times \\(\\sum_{A,B}v_Av_BZ_N(w)\\). This proves finiteness, positivity, and invertibility.</p><p>Equation (5.1) gives \\(-R(-1)=g(-1)^tM^{-1}f(0)\\). Expanding minors gives numerator\n\\[\n\\frac1{(N-1)!}\\int\n\\det f(s_1,\\ldots,s_{N-1},0)\n\\det g(s_1,\\ldots,s_{N-1},-1)\n\\prod_i d\\mu(s_i).\n\\]\nInsert the pole expansions; the polynomial cofactor identity gives \\(Z_N(w)K_N^w(0,-1)\\), with the two external denominators. Both external nodes lie below the integration nodes, so the common-sign integrals are nonnegative.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n1\\ge\\pi_B(-1)\\ge\\prod_b(1-1/b)=\\sin\\lambda/\\lambda&gt;0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly over \\(A,B\\), \\( K_N^w(0,-1)\\le C\\sqrt{\\log(2N)}\\,N^{3/4}. \\) For every degree-\\(j\\) orthogonal polynomial of any conditional weight, \\( 1\\le\\frac{P_j(-1)}{P_j(0)} \\le C(j+1)^{3/4}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Uniform reference-kernel comparisons.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use conditional weights \\(w\\) from Lemma 6.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu_\\alpha\\) be the pushforward to \\(s=p^2\\) of \\(dy/\\cosh(\\pi y)\\), where \\(y=\\alpha p/\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  w_\\infty(p)=\\frac{x(2\\cosh2x-1)}{\\sinh8x}\n  \\]\n  be the all-poles density before pushforward.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over \\(A,B\\),\n\\[\nK_N^w(0,-1)\\le C\\sqrt{\\log(2N)}\\,N^{3/4}.\n\\]\nFor every degree-\\(j\\) orthogonal polynomial of any conditional weight,\n\\[\n1\\le\\frac{P_j(-1)}{P_j(0)}\n\\le C(j+1)^{3/4}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Zeros are eigenvalues of multiplication by \\(s\\) compressed to lower-degree polynomials. An increasing likelihood ratio raises every Rayleigh quotient by monotone tilting and hence raises zeros by min-max. Diagonal kernels satisfy\n\\[\nK_d^w(u,u)=\n\\sup_{\\deg Q&lt;d,\\ Q\\ne0}\n\\frac{|Q(u)|^2}{\\int|Q|^2\\,dw}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\nu_\\alpha\\), degree-\\(j\\) polynomials in \\(s\\) are, up to sign, the coefficients of order \\(2j\\) in\n\\[\n(1-v^2)^{-1/2}\n\\left(\\frac{1+v}{1-v}\\right)^{iy}.\n\\]\nIntegrating products gives \\(1/(1+uv)\\), using the Fourier transform of \\(1/\\cosh\\pi y\\). Their magnitudes at \\(0\\) are of order \\((j+1)^{-1/2}\\); at \\(-1\\), for \\(r=\\alpha/\\pi\\in(1/2,1)\\), they are of order \\((j+1)^{r-1/2}\\). The latter follows by convolution from\n\\[\n(1-v)^{-1/2-r}(1+v)^{r-1/2}.\n\\]</p><p>The ratio \\(w_\\infty/\\nu_{6\\lambda}\\) is increasing; up to constants it is\n\\[\n\\frac{x(2-1/\\cosh2x)}{\\tanh2x+\\tanh6x}.\n\\]\nRemoving poles increases this upward tilt, and \\(w\\) uniformly dominates a constant multiple of \\(\\nu_{6\\lambda}\\). Conversely, \\(w/\\nu_{2\\lambda}\\) decreases, since the corresponding \\(\\mu\\)-ratio is proportional to\n\\[\n(2\\cosh2x-1)/\\cosh4x.\n\\]\nThe diagonal extremal bound and Cauchy–Schwarz give the kernel upper bound. Comparing zeros with \\(\\nu_{6\\lambda}\\) gives the polynomial-ratio bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.3 (Low-pole saturation)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb P(\\mathcal E_J)=1-o(1). \\) For either subset, missing any such pole has probability \\(o(1)\\), uniformly conditional on the other subset.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/2\\), put \\(J=N^{1-\\delta}\\), and let \\(\\mathcal E_J\\) mean that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) each contain every pole whose index \\(l\\le J\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\mathcal E_J)=1-o(1).\n\\]\nFor either subset, missing any such pole has probability \\(o(1)\\), uniformly conditional on the other subset.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the other subset. If \\(j\\le J\\) is missing, replace the smallest occupied \\(k\\ge2J\\) by \\(j\\). Such a \\(k\\) exists since the subset has \\(N/2\\) members. For fixed \\(k\\), replacement is injective. The old-to-new weight ratio is\n\\[\n(k/j)^2\n\\prod_{l\\ne k}\\left(\\frac{k^2-l^2}{j^2-l^2}\\right)^2\n\\mathbb E_{\\rm new}\\prod_{i=1}^N\n\\frac{s_i+(8j)^2}{s_i+(8k)^2}.\n\\]\nThe non-expectation factors cost at most\n\\(\\exp(CJ)(k/J)^{CJ}\\): indices above \\(k\\) cost nothing, and unit spacing below \\(2J\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_l\\bigl[\\log^+(J/l)+\\log^+(J/|j-l|)\\bigr]\\le CJ.\n\\]</p><p>The expectation is a ratio of degree-\\(N\\) monic orthogonal-polynomial values for \\(w_{\\rm new}/(s+(8k)^2)\\). Averaging characteristic polynomials against a normalized Vandermonde-square ensemble gives its monic orthogonal polynomial by antisymmetrization. This extra denominator preserves decreasing likelihood ratio relative to \\(\\nu_{2\\lambda}\\). Zero comparison therefore bounds the ratio by the reference ratio.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nc_M(R)=[v^M](1-v)^{-1/2-R}(1+v)^{-1/2+R},\n\\qquad M=2N.\n\\]\nThe ratio is bounded by\n\\[\n\\exp(CJ)(k/J)^{CJ}\\frac{c_M(2j)}{c_M(2k)}. \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nCoefficients increase with \\(R\\), since the generating function contains \\(\\exp(2R\\operatorname{atanh}v)\\), and\n\\[\nc_M(2k)\\ge\\binom{M+2k-1}{2k-1}.\n\\]\nFor \\(2J\\le k\\le N^{1-\\delta/2}\\), bound the numerator on radius \\(1-k/N\\) by\n\\(\\exp(O(k))(CN/k)^{2j+1}\\); the denominator is at least \\((N/k)^{2k-1}\\). Its logarithmic advantage is \\(\\gtrsim\\delta k\\log N\\), absorbing replacement costs. For \\(N^{1-\\delta/2}&lt;k&lt;N\\), the denominator is at least \\(\\exp(c_\\delta N^{1-\\delta/2}\\log N)\\), whereas costs and numerator are at most \\(\\exp(CJ\\log N)\\). For \\(k\\ge N\\), the binomial gives \\(\\exp(cN)(k/N)^M\\), summable to infinity.</p><p>Thus (5.6) sums to \\(o(1/J)\\), uniformly in \\(j\\le J\\). A union bound over both low-index sets proves the assertion without independence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( -R(-1)=N^{3/4+o(1)}. \\) Consequently \\( d_N:=\\frac1{2\\cos3\\lambda}-G_N =N^{-1/4+o(1)} \\) for even \\(N\\).", "statement": "<p>Lower kernel bound and susceptibility exponent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(R\\) from Lemma 6.1 and the pole law above.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n-R(-1)=N^{3/4+o(1)}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nd_N:=\\frac1{2\\cos3\\lambda}-G_N\n=N^{-1/4+o(1)}\n\\]\nfor even \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(\\mathcal E_J\\),\n\\[\nw/w_\\infty\\le\\exp(Cp^2/J).\n\\]\nFix \\(\\alpha'&lt;6\\lambda\\) close to \\(6\\lambda\\). On \\(|p|\\le\\epsilon J\\), with \\(\\epsilon\\) small, \\(w\\le C\\nu_{\\alpha'}\\). Put \\(d=\\lfloor N^{1-2\\delta}\\rfloor\\). Uniformly over saturated pole sets,\n\\[\n\\int|Q|^2\\,dw\\le C\\int|Q|^2\\,d\\nu_{\\alpha'}\n\\qquad(\\deg Q&lt;d). \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nFor the tails, the reference generating function on a small radius gives\n\\[\n|Q(p^2)|^2\\le\\|Q\\|_{L^2(\\nu_{\\alpha'})}^2\n\\exp(C_rd+C_r|p|).\n\\]\nSince \\(w(p)\\le Ce^{-4\\lambda|p|}\\), choose the radius to retain exponential decay. The tail \\(|p|&gt;\\epsilon J\\) costs at most\n\\[\nC e^{C_rd-c\\epsilon J}\\|Q\\|^2,\n\\]\nand \\(d=o(J)\\).</p><p>All zeros are positive, so \\(P_j(0)P_j(-1)&gt;0\\). Lemma 6.3 and (5.7) give on \\(\\mathcal E_J\\)\n\\[\nK_N^w(0,-1)\n\\ge C^{-1}d^{-3/4}K_d^w(-1,-1)\n\\ge c\\,d^{2\\alpha'/\\pi-3/4}.\n\\]\nUse Lemma 6.4, bounded external denominators in (5.5), and the uniform upper bound. Let \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), \\(\\alpha'\\uparrow6\\lambda\\). Equation (5.3) gives the excluded-mass exponent. Since \\(G_N\\) tends to the flux upper bound, the full rightward arch mass equals that bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The height and diameter tails are \\(r^{-1/4+o(1)}\\), and the missing strip arch mass has the same exponent.", "statement": "<p>Comparison of cylinder exclusion, spatial extent, and height.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For rightward half-plane arches from \\(0\\), let \\(D&gt;0\\) be endpoint displacement and \\(Y\\) maximum height.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  A=\\sqrt3/2,\\quad B=1/2,\\quad\n  M_\\pm=\\max(Ax\\pm By),\n  \\]\n  and write \\(\\mathsf P\\) for the unnormalized weighted law.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U(r)=\\mathsf P(M_+\\ge r)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The height and diameter tails are \\(r^{-1/4+o(1)}\\), and the missing strip arch mass has the same exponent.</p>", "proof": "<p>At suitable tiling-grid levels,\n\\[\n\\mathsf P(M_\\pm-AD\\ge r)\n=q_\\pm\\mathsf P(M_\\pm\\ge r),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nq_\\pm=\n\\frac{\\sin(\\sigma(\\pi-\\varphi_\\pm))}\n     {\\sin(\\sigma(\\pi+\\varphi_\\pm))},\n\\qquad\n(\\varphi_+,\\varphi_-)=(\\pi/3,2\\pi/3),\n\\quad 0&lt;q_-&lt;q_+&lt;1.\n\\]\nTo derive these, add the tilted cut to a large convex exhaustion and subtract the two flux identities. The lost phased sum lies on the new-exit ray. Non-bottom exits vanish because bottom arch mass saturates flux; reversal and translation identify the leftward terms with shifted maxima. Taking the rotated imaginary part gives the identities.</p><p>From \\(d_N\\), endpoint tails satisfy\n\\(\\mathsf P(D\\ge r)\\le r^{-1/4+o(1)}\\). A cylinder-excluded arch has horizontal range of order \\(N\\), either from displacement or from vertices differing by a nonzero period. Reflection with reversal turns far-left excursions into far-right ones, giving\n\\(U(r)\\ge r^{-1/4-o(1)}\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(\\rho&lt;1\\) sufficiently close to \\(1\\),\n\\[\nU(r)\\le\\mathsf P(D\\ge c_\\rho r)+q_+U(\\rho r).\n\\]\nChoose \\(q_+\\rho^{-1/4}&lt;1\\) and iterate, obtaining \\(U(r)=r^{-1/4+o(1)}\\). This bounds the full spatial tail.</p><p>Use \\(M_-\\le M_+\\le M_-+2BY\\) and both ray identities to get\n\\[\nU(r)\\le q_+^{-1}\\mathsf P(Y\\ge c'_\\rho r)\n+(q_-/q_+)U(\\rho r).\n\\]\nChoose \\((q_-/q_+)\\rho^{-1/4}&lt;1\\). Iterate until \\(\\rho^kr\\) is of order \\(r^{1-\\epsilon}\\). The remainder is negligible relative to \\(U(r)\\), and monotonicity bounds the height terms by a constant times\n\\(\\mathsf P(Y\\ge cr^{1-\\epsilon})\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathsf P(Y\\ge cr^{1-\\epsilon})\\ge r^{-1/4-o(1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\). The diameter exponent follows, and (2.1) identifies the missing strip arch mass, up to constants and layer rounding, with the height tail.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\mathcal P\\) has the finite neutral color formula in Lemma 7.1.", "statement": "<p>Finite color formula and small-cycle representation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the generic contraction of Proposition 5 and define\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(X;\\kappa)\n=\\frac{S_N(X)^2X_{n,m}(X;\\kappa)}\n       {\\prod_{i&lt;j}(x_ix_j)}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\mathcal P\\) has the finite neutral color formula in Lemma 7.1. At balanced homogeneous sites it has the two-color small-cycle integral representation in Lemma 7.2. These are the numerator representations used for asymptotics.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 identifies the numerator through the exact interpolation data of Proposition 5. Lemma 7.2 reorganizes its homogeneous balanced color sum into a finite small-cycle integral. Object length counts virtual variables, not visited vertices of a walk.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\mathcal P\\) is the sum, over neutral assignments, of the products of all site and pair factors.", "statement": "<p>Finite color formula.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assign \\(a_i\\in\\{1,0,-1\\}\\) with \\(\\sum_i a_i=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Block type \\(P=A,B\\) has sign \\(s_A=1,s_B=-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Site activities satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_0^P=1,\\qquad\n  \\lambda_+^P\\lambda_-^P=\\sqrt2-2,\\qquad\n  \\lambda_+^A/\\lambda_+^B=T\\kappa.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A pair of source type \\(P\\), target type \\(Q\\), ratio \\(r=x_{\\rm target}/x_{\\rm source}\\), and labels \\(a,b\\), has factor\n  \\[\n  l_{ab}^{PQ}(r)\n  =u_{ab}^{PQ}r^{-1-ab}\n   \\prod_{j\\bmod8}(r-t^j)^{d_{ab}^{PQ}(j)}.\n  \\]\n  The multiplier is \\(t^{-s_Pb}\\) if \\(a=0\\), and\n  \\(t^{s_Qa(1-2b^2)}\\) otherwise.</p><p class=\"li\">For unit divisor arrays \\(e_j\\), write \\(*\\) for index negation, and set\n  \\[\n  H=e_2+e_3+e_5+e_6,\\quad\n  J=e_3+e_5+e_6-e_0,\\quad\n  L=e_5+e_6-e_0-e_1.\n  \\]\n  In label order \\(+,0,-\\),\n  \\[\n  d^{AA}=\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  H&amp;J&amp;L\\\\\n  J^*&amp;H-e_1-e_{-1}&amp;J\\\\\n  L^*&amp;J^*&amp;H\n  \\end{pmatrix},\n  \\qquad\n  d^{AB}=2\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  e_5+e_6&amp;e_5&amp;0\\\\\n  e_5&amp;e_4&amp;e_3\\\\\n  0&amp;e_3&amp;e_2+e_3\n  \\end{pmatrix}.\n  \\]\n  Obtain \\(BB,BA\\) by reversing both labels in \\(AA,AB\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\mathcal P\\) is the sum, over neutral assignments, of the products of all site and pair factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Verify the characterization of Lemma 5.2 after restoring the monomial denominator. Normalize source labels by \\(g=s_Pa\\), \\(h'=s_Pb\\), \\(\\epsilon=s_Ps_Q\\). The arrays have total sum \\(2+2gh'\\), and pair factors are reciprocal under exchanging endpoints.</p><p>Equal-site poles cancel between exchanged unequal labels. At ratio \\(t\\), the polar assignments \\((s,-s),(0,0)\\) cancel including activities. At \\(T\\), only \\((s,-s)\\) remains, with local activity multiplier \\(2-\\sqrt2\\). At \\(t^2\\), pairs \\((s,0),(0,-s),(s,-s)\\) coalesce with twice the single activity.</p><p>For a target label \\(c\\), put\n\\[\nB_{ab,c}(r;k)=l_{ac}^{PQ}(r)l_{bc}^{PQ}(r/t^k).\n\\]\nThe table gives\n\\[\nB_{s,-s,c}(r;1)=t^{3sc}B_{00,c}(r;1),\n\\]\n\\[\nB_{s,-s,c}(r;3)\n=T^{sc}\\frac{(r-t^5)^2(r-t^6)^2}{Tr^2},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_{ab,c}(r;2)=\n\\frac{(r-t^5)^2}{tr}l_{a+b,c}^{PQ}(r/t)\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;a+b=\\pm1,\\\\\nt^{3sc},&amp;(a,b)=(s,-s).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThese follow from the divisor identities\n\\[\nD_++\\tau_1D_-=(1+\\tau_1)D_0,\\qquad\nD_++\\tau_3D_-=2(e_5+e_6),\n\\]\n\\[\nD_g+\\tau_2D_{h'}=2e_5+\\tau_1D_{g+h'}\n\\]\non the allowed pairs, and comparison of leading phases. Neutrality cancels spectator phases. The relevant local values are\n\\[\nl_{00}^{PP}/l_{s,-s}^{PP}\\to2-\\sqrt2\\quad(r\\to t),\n\\quad l_{s,-s}^{PP}(T)=-1,\n\\]\nand \\(2,2,-(2+\\sqrt2)\\) at \\(t^2\\).</p><p>At extreme parameters, neutrality gives degree \\(N-1-a^2\\) in either divergent direction; only label zero remains at top order, with phases canceling.</p><p>At balanced nested cups, cross factors force each matched label sum to be zero. For matched labels \\(a,-a\\) and \\(b,-b\\), the inter-match factor is\n\\[\nl_{ab}^{AA}(r)^2\nl_{a,-b}^{AB}(Tr)l_{b,-a}^{AB}(T/r)\n=\nr^{-4}\\prod_{d\\in\\{2,3,5,6\\}}(r-t^d)^2,\n\\]\nindependent of labels; each local pair contributes\n\\((2-\\sqrt2)\\kappa^a\\). Thus the balanced initial condition also agrees. Uniqueness proves the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The gas summed with inverse factorials of indistinguishable-object counts, \\(E,D\\) activities \\(A_P,B_P\\), and \\(M\\le2n\\), equals \\(\\mathcal P/B_n\\).", "statement": "<p>Balanced small-cycle gas.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take two homogeneous blocks of size \\(n\\), and put\n  \\[\n  D_0=2+\\sqrt2,\\qquad\n  B_n=(2D_0)^{n^2}D_0^{-2n}\\kappa^n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In each color, an \\(E\\)-object has one virtual variable \\(z\\); a \\(D\\)-object has two, \\((z,z/t)\\). The common total virtual length in the two colors is \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Tops \\(z\\) run around small positive circles about \\(1\\), with measure \\(dz/(2\\pi iz)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each virtual variable carries \\(h(r)^n\\), and distinct objects carry all constituent pair factors, with\n  \\[\n  h(r)=\\frac{(r+t)^2}{(r-1)(r-t^2)},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  K_s(r)=\\frac{(r-1)^2(1+r^2)}{tr(r-t)(r-1/t)},\n  \\qquad\n  K_x(r)=\\frac{tr(1+r)^2}{(1+\\sqrt2r+r^2)^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Activities are\n  \\[\n  A_P=\\frac1{a_0\\lambda_{s_P}^P},\n  \\quad a_0=\\frac{t^2}{1-t^2},\n  \\quad\n  B_P=-A_P^2\\operatorname*{Res}_{r=t}(K_s(r)/r).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The gas summed with inverse factorials of indistinguishable-object counts, \\(E,D\\) activities \\(A_P,B_P\\), and \\(M\\le2n\\), equals \\(\\mathcal P/B_n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First separate base sites slightly, preserving block sizes, and divide by the corresponding default-label term. Replace \\(h^n\\) by products of\n\\[\np_s(r)=\\frac{t^2r}{(r-1)(r-t^2)},\\qquad\np_x(r)=t^6(r-t^5)^2/r.\n\\]\nResidues select distinct sites of the relevant color: same-color combined kernels have coincidence zero orders \\(2,1,2\\) for \\(EE,ED,DD\\).</p><p>A length-\\(i\\) object changes default label \\(s_P\\) to \\(s_P(1-i)\\). Against an unchanged site, the pair-table ratio is the corresponding string of \\(p\\)'s times \\(t^{3s_Ps_Q1_{i=2}}\\). Between changed sites, the correction is the string of \\(K\\)'s times\n\\[\nt^{-3s_Ps_Q(i1_{j=2}+j1_{i=2})}.\n\\]\nNeutrality leaves net \\(t^3\\) per \\(D\\)-object. Self-site residues are \\(a_0\\), or \\(a_0p_s(1/t)\\) for \\(D\\), and\n\\[\nB_P=\\frac{t^3\\lambda_{-s_P}^P}\n          {\\lambda_{s_P}^Pa_0p_s(1/t)}.\n\\]</p><p>Equivalently, the pair correction divisor is the string of\n\\[\nK_+^\\#=2e_0+e_2+e_6-e_1-e_7,\n\\qquad\nK_-^\\#=2e_4-2e_3-2e_5,\n\\]\nwith shifts \\(\\sum_{u&lt;i,v&lt;j}\\tau_{u-v}\\), and the leading-phase difference is\n\\[\n\\epsilon[-ij+ij(j-i)/2-3(i1_{j=2}+j1_{i=2})]\n\\pmod8.\n\\]\nNo other poles intervene. Summing residues gives the color formula, and passing to homogeneous sites gives the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 6.1 (Pfaffian denominator)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For an absolute \\(c>0\\), \\( S_{2n}(1,\\ldots,1) \\ge cD_0^{-n}(4D_0^2/9)^{n^2}n^{5/48}. \\) The logarithmic error in this one-sided estimate is bounded.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(D_0=2+\\sqrt2\\), \\(\\delta=3/8\\), and \\(S_{2n}\\) be the empty polynomial of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nS_{2n}(1,\\ldots,1)\n\\ge cD_0^{-n}(4D_0^2/9)^{n^2}n^{5/48}.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic error in this one-sided estimate is bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiply the exact reference normalization from Lemma 8.1 by the determinant-ratio lower bound from Lemma 8.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( S_{2n}(1)= (D_0/2)^{\\binom{2n}2} \\frac{\\det[\\int p^{2j+2k}W(p)\\,dp]_{j,k=0}^{n-1}} {\\prod_{j=0}^{2n-1}j!}. \\) Replacing \\(W\\) by \\(W_0\\) gives exactly \\( D_0^{-n}(4D_0^2/9)^{n^2}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Homogeneous moment determinant and reference normalization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Define\n\\[\nW(p)=\\frac{p\\cosh(\\lambda p)}{2\\sinh(4\\lambda p)}\n=W_0(p)e^{f(p)},\n\\]\n\\[\nW_0(p)=\\frac p{4\\sinh(\\delta\\pi p)},\n\\qquad\nf(p)=\\log\\!\\left(1+\\frac1{2\\cosh(\\pi p/4)}\\right).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nS_{2n}(1)=\n(D_0/2)^{\\binom{2n}2}\n\\frac{\\det[\\int p^{2j+2k}W(p)\\,dp]_{j,k=0}^{n-1}}\n     {\\prod_{j=0}^{2n-1}j!}.\n\\]\nReplacing \\(W\\) by \\(W_0\\) gives exactly\n\\[\nD_0^{-n}(4D_0^2/9)^{n^2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At variables \\(e^{2ia},e^{2ib}\\), the Pfaffian kernel is\n\\[\n\\frac{i\\sin2(a-b)}{\\cos2(a-b)+1/\\sqrt2}\n=i\\,\\operatorname{pv}\\int e^{(a-b)p}W(p)\\,\\frac{dp}{p}.\n\\]\nTake the homogeneous Taylor/Vandermonde limit. Multilinearity selects Taylor indices \\(0,\\ldots,2n-1\\); pairing evens and odds gives the displayed determinant, with signs canceling.</p><p>For \\(W_0\\), use \\(y=\\delta p\\) and\n\\(C=\\int W_0=1/(8\\delta^2)\\). Even coefficients of\n\\[\n(1-v)^{-1-iy}(1+v)^{-1+iy}\n\\]\nare real orthogonal polynomials, with squared norms \\(C(m+1)\\) at indices \\(m=0,2,\\ldots\\). Integrating products uses the transform of \\(2y\\,dy/\\sinh\\pi y\\) and gives \\((1+vu)^{-2}\\). Monic squared norms are\n\\[\n\\frac{Cm!(m+1)!}{(2\\delta)^{2m}}.\n\\]\nTheir product in the determinant gives the exact reference factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The logarithm of the \\(W/W_0\\) moment-determinant ratio is at least \\( \\frac5{48}\\log n-O(1). \\)", "statement": "<p>Trace lower bound for the denominator correction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(f,W_0\\) from Lemma 8.1 and the reference even-polynomial space of dimension \\(n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The logarithm of the \\(W/W_0\\) moment-determinant ratio is at least\n\\[\n\\frac5{48}\\log n-O(1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Scalar Jensen in an orthonormal eigenbasis of the positive Gram matrix bounds its log determinant below by the trace of multiplication by \\(f\\).</p><p>For large even \\(m\\) and \\(|y|\\le c_1\\log m\\), the reference generating coefficient is\n\\[\n2\\Re\\!\\left[\n2^{-1+iy}m^{iy}H(y)(1+O((1+y^2)/m))\n\\right]+O(m^{-1/2}),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nH(y)=\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}k^{-iy}\\prod_{j=1}^k(1+iy/j),\n\\qquad\n|H(y)|^2=\\frac{\\sinh\\pi y}{\\pi y}.\n\\]\nSubtract the two endpoint singular terms from the generating function. The remainder's derivative is bounded by\n\\[\nC(1+y^2)e^{C|y|}/|1-v^2|.\n\\]\nOn radius \\(1-1/m\\), the angular integral is \\(O(\\log m)\\); coefficient extraction gives the additive error for small enough \\(c_1\\). Logarithmic-product tails give the multiplicative error.</p><p>Drop \\(|y|&gt;c_1\\log m\\) by positivity. After cancellation of \\(|H|^2\\) with the weight, the contribution per even index is at least\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\pi(m+1)}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(y/\\delta)\n\\left[1+\\Re\\!\\left((2m)^{2iy}H(y)/\\overline{H(y)}\\right)\\right]dy\n\\]\nup to summable errors. Two integrations by parts make the oscillatory integral \\(O((\\log m)^{-2})\\); the logarithmic derivatives of the phase have polynomial growth.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(p)\\,dp=5\\pi/9,\n\\]\nby expanding\n\\[\n\\log(1-e^{-3u})-\\log(1-e^{-u})-\\log(1+e^{-2u}),\n\\qquad u=\\pi|p|/4.\n\\]\nThe even-index harmonic sum gives \\((5/48)\\log n-O(1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a numerator \\(\\mathcal N_n(q_{\\rm rad},s)\\), analytic in a fixed disk about zero nome, with \\( \\mathcal N_n(0,s)=S_{2n}(1)^2X_{n,n}(e^s). \\) Its fixed-\\(M\\) sectors admit the radial deformation, Fourier …", "statement": "<p>Radial periodization and upper-circle resummation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Start from the small-cycle gas of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=\\pi/4,\\quad \\kappa=e^s,\\quad\n  x=\\log(z/t),\\quad q_{\\rm rad}=e^{-L/2},\\quad P=q_{\\rm rad}^2.\n  \\]\n  The auxiliary spectral period \\(L\\) is distinct from physical circumference \\(2n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  S(x)=2\\sinh(x/2)\\prod_{a\\ge1}(1-P^ae^x)(1-P^ae^{-x}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Replace kernels by products \\(\\prod_jS(x-i\\alpha j)^{d_j}\\), with\n  \\[\n  h:\\ d_{\\pm1}=-1,\\ d_{\\pm4}=1;\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  K_s:\\ d_0=2,\\ d_{\\pm1}=-1,\\ d_{\\pm2}=1;\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  K_x:\\ d_{\\pm3}=-2,\\ d_{\\pm4}=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  C_L^2=\\lim_{x\\to0}K_s(x)/x^2,\\qquad\n  k_L=\\operatorname*{Res}_{x=i\\alpha}K_s(x),\n  \\]\n  with the square root positive at zero nome. Use \\(E\\)-activity\n  \\(a_L=-iC_Le^{-s/2}\\) and \\(D\\)-activity \\(-k_La_L^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a numerator \\(\\mathcal N_n(q_{\\rm rad},s)\\), analytic in a fixed disk about zero nome, with\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_n(0,s)=S_{2n}(1)^2X_{n,n}(e^s).\n\\]\nIts fixed-\\(M\\) sectors admit the radial deformation, Fourier representation, winding-circle expansion, and Hubbard averaging in Lemmas 9.1–9.4.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 9.1 constructs the analytic periodized numerator without changing its value at zero nome. Lemma 9.2 supplies exact Fourier, mean, and self-factor conventions. Lemmas 9.3–9.4 eliminate upper ensembles through a winding-sensitive circle expansion and justified Gaussian averaging. The remaining variables are lower gas objects and holes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The integrand is elliptic in each object's top coordinate.", "statement": "<p>Elliptic deformation and the two radial edges.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A \\(D\\)-object has constituents \\(x,x-i\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Multiply the integrand by \\(\\Theta(C)^2\\), where\n  \\[\n  C=\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_\\nu q_\\nu x_\\nu\\right),\n  \\qquad\n  \\Theta(C)=\\sum_{b\\in\\mathbb Z}e^{-Lb^2/2}C^b,\n  \\]\n  and \\(q_\\nu=\\pm1\\) is the gas color.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) as this deformed gas, summed over \\(0\\le M\\le2n\\), multiplied by \\(B_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The integrand is elliptic in each object's top coordinate. The numerator has the analyticity and zero-nome value in Proposition 9. Every fixed sector \\(M&gt;2n\\), if included, vanishes.</p><p>At fixed \\(M\\) and large real \\(L\\), small cycles deform into:</p><p class=\"li\">upper \\(E\\)-variables \\(U\\) at shift \\(0\\), with reversed sign;</p><p class=\"li\">lower \\(E\\)-variables, renamed \\(L\\), at shift \\(2+\\ell\\), \\(\\ell=1/2\\);</p><p class=\"li\">lower \\(D\\)-tops at shift \\(2+d\\), \\(d=-1/8\\),\nwhere a shift \\(\\theta\\) means \\(x=r-i\\alpha\\theta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The identity\n\\[\nS(x+L)=-e^{x+L/2}S(x)\n\\]\nmakes the pair multiplier under shifting an object of charge \\(p_i\\)\n\\[\nC^{-2p_i}e^{-p_i^2L};\n\\]\n\\(\\Theta(C)^2\\) cancels it by neutrality.</p><p>At zero nome the stripped pair phases and activities agree with Proposition 7:\n\\[\nC_\\infty^2=D_0,\\qquad tA_AA_B=-D_0/\\kappa.\n\\]\nNormal product convergence gives analyticity on a contour size and nome disk independent of \\(n\\). Split the external poles slightly. Same-color coincidence zeros of orders \\(2,1,2\\) force distinct top-pole selections, at most \\(n\\) per color, each of length at most two. Thus sectors \\(M&gt;2n\\) vanish, and the zero-nome value is the original numerator.</p><p>Expand a small cycle as lower minus upper at shifts \\(1\\pm\\epsilon_0\\). Move upper \\(E\\)'s to shift zero. A same-color lower \\(E\\)-pole creates a two-string with coefficient \\(-k_La_L^2\\); these cancel upper \\(D\\)'s. Disjoint merger matchings and factorials give, for total upper two-string count \\(d'\\), the relative coefficient\n\\[\n\\sum_{r'=0}^{d'}\n\\frac{(-1)^{d'-r'}}{r'!(d'-r')!},\n\\]\nzero unless \\(d'=0\\).</p><p>There are no further poles against \\(E,D\\): the potential same-color poles cancel against string zeros. Move lower contours together to the stated shifts. Opposite-color distances stay below \\(3\\), and lower top differences stay below \\(1\\), so no pole is crossed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Both eigenvalues of \\(A(v)\\) are positive for \\(v>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fourier and zero-mode bookkeeping before upper resummation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\varepsilon=2\\pi\\alpha/L\\), \\(\\varphi=2\\pi r/L\\), \\(y=e^{i\\varphi}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For constituent pair divisor \\(d_j\\), target-minus-source shift \\(\\theta\\), and \\(p=\\sum_jd_j/2\\), define\n  \\[\n  W(v)=\\sum_jd_j\n  \\frac{\\exp(v((j+\\theta)\\bmod8-4))}{2\\sinh4v}\n  =\\frac p{4v}+W^{[0]}+W^{[1]}v+O(v^2),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  k=\\sum_jd_j\\left(\\frac12+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{j+\\theta}{8}\\right\\rfloor\\right),\n  \\]\n  using half-averages on a wall.</p><p class=\"li\">Sum constituent arrays to get lower-lower \\(W_{ij}\\), upper-to-lower columns \\(J_i^\\pm\\), upper color matrix \\(A\\), and external coefficients \\(H,h_i^\\pm\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In particular,\n  \\[\n  H=\\frac{1-\\cosh3v}{\\sinh4v},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  A_{ss}=\\frac{\\cosh4v-\\cosh3v+\\cosh2v}{\\sinh4v},\n  \\qquad\n  A_{sx}=\\frac{1-2\\cosh v}{\\sinh4v}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both eigenvalues of \\(A(v)\\) are positive for \\(v&gt;0\\). The compensated pair kernel has winding \\((-y_j/y_i)^k\\), mean logarithm\n\\[\npL/6+p\\alpha^2\\theta^2/L-\\varepsilon W^{[1]},\n\\]\nand positive-frequency log coefficient \\(-W(m\\varepsilon)/m\\).</p><p>Writing \\(u\\) for upper color counts, the upper variables become independent circular-unitary ensembles with the quadratic and linear Fourier insertions described in the proof.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiply a pair kernel by \\(\\exp[-p(x_j-x_i)^2/L]\\). Expand\n\\[\nS(r-i\\alpha u)\n=\\operatorname{sgn}(r)e^{(|r|-i\\alpha u\\operatorname{sgn}r)/2}\n\\prod_{l\\in\\mathbb Z}\n(1-e^{-|r-lL|}e^{i\\alpha u\\operatorname{sgn}(r-lL)}).\n\\]\nSigns cancel; the compensated log jump gives the winding. Means are computed with the Bernoulli polynomial \\(B_2\\), and nonzero frequencies with the Fourier series of \\(e^{\\omega(x-\\pi)}/(2\\sinh\\pi\\omega)\\). Per shifted factor, the coefficient calculation uses\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\omega^2}+\\frac{\\beta-\\pi}{\\omega}\n+2\\sum_{a\\ge1}\n\\frac{\\cos(a\\beta)+(\\omega/a)\\sin(a\\beta)}{a^2+\\omega^2}\n=\n\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n\\frac{e^{\\omega(\\beta-\\pi)}}{2\\sinh\\pi\\omega}.\n\\]</p><p>Poisson-transform the two theta factors, with indices \\(b_1,b_2\\), sum \\(b\\). Put\n\\[\np_i=\\sum_{\\nu\\in i}q_\\nu,\\qquad z_0=\\sum_\\nu q_\\nu\\theta_\\nu.\n\\]\nThe scalar mean is \\(2\\pi/L\\) times\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left\\{-\\frac\\varepsilon2\n\\left[\n\\frac{(z_0+4b)^2}4+4(b_1-b_2)^2\n+[v]\\left\\{\nu^tAu+2u^t(nH1+\\sum_iJ_i^+)\n+\\sum_{ij}W_{ij}+2n\\sum_ih_i^+\n\\right\\}\n\\right]\\right\\}.\n\\]\nRaw Gaussian compensation sums to\n\\[\n[-(\\sum_\\nu q_\\nu x_\\nu)^2+\\#D\\,\\alpha^2]/L,\n\\]\nand cancels the modular theta square; pair offsets contribute\n\\(\\alpha^2(-z_0^2-\\#D)/L\\).</p><p>Upper means and self factors give Haar-circle Vandermonde squares. The upper winding is \\(-e_c-bq_c\\), where \\(e_c\\) counts lower objects. If \\(T_\\pm\\) are upper sums of \\(y^{\\pm m}\\), their insertion per mode is\n\\[\n\\frac1m\\left[\nT_-^t(1-A)T_+\n-T_-^t(nH1+\\sum_iJ_i^+y_i^m)\n-(nH1+\\sum_iJ_i^-y_i^{-m})^tT_+\n\\right].\n\\]\nThe remaining log is\n\\[\n-\\frac1m\\left[\n\\sum_{ij}W_{ij}y_j^my_i^{-m}\n-\\sum_iW_{ii}(\\infty)(1-e^{-v})\n+n\\sum_i(h_i^+y_i^m+h_i^-y_i^{-m})\n\\right].\n\\]\nOutside phases are\n\\[\n\\prod_i y_i^{-bp_i}(-y_i)^{u_{c(i)}-n|p_i|}\n\\prod_{i&lt;j}(-y_j/y_i)^{k_{ij}}.\n\\]</p><p>Lower self corrections are\n\\[\n\\Gamma_i=-p_i^2L/12+\\varepsilon W_{ii}^{[1]}/2\n+\\sum_{m&gt;0}\n\\frac{W_{ii}(m\\varepsilon)-W_{ii}(\\infty)(1-e^{-m\\varepsilon})}{m}\n=O(1);\n\\]\nsubtracting \\(p_i^2/(4m\\varepsilon)\\) cancels linear growth. The upper self correction is exactly\n\\[\n-\\log(LC_L/(2\\pi)),\n\\]\nbecause the compensated same-color kernel divided by\n\\(|1-e^{i\\varphi}|^2\\) tends to \\((LC_L/(2\\pi))^2\\). It cancels the signed activity/measure conversion. The formulas continue to large complex \\(L\\) with bounded imaginary part.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The expectation equals the absolutely convergent hole expansion with scalar prefactor \\( (-1)^{ud}\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{m>0}m[y^m]V_+[y^{-m}]V_-\\right) \\) and hole factors …", "statement": "<p>Winding circle-determinant expansion.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_+(y)\\), \\(V_-(y)\\) have strictly positive and negative powers, analytic on an annulus containing the unit circle.</p><p class=\"li\">In the circular-unitary expectation of size \\(u\\), insert \\(y^{-d}e^{V_++V_-}\\), with \\(d\\in\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Holes have sign \\(\\tau=+1\\) on an outer circle and \\(\\tau=-1\\) on an inner circle; their counts \\(r,l\\) satisfy \\(r-l=d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use normalized positive circle measures divided by factorials, and\n  \\[\n  E(a,b)=\\frac{(a-b)^2}{ab}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The expectation equals the absolutely convergent hole expansion with scalar prefactor\n\\[\n(-1)^{ud}\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{m&gt;0}m[y^m]V_+[y^{-m}]V_-\\right)\n\\]\nand hole factors\n\\[\n\\prod_hY_h^{-u\\tau_h}\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_h\\tau_h(V_-(Y_h)-V_+(Y_h))\\right)\n\\prod_{h&lt;h'}E(Y_h,Y_{h'})^{\\tau_h\\tau_{h'}},\n\\]\ntimes a sign depending on \\(r,l,d\\), not on \\(u\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First use finitely many times formally. Vandermonde minors and circle integration give, for \\(d\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell(\\lambda)\\le u}s_{\\lambda+(d^u)}(V_+)s_\\lambda(V_-).\n\\]\nComplementary inverse Toeplitz minors transpose partitions, giving\n\\[\n(-1)^{ud}\\sum_{\\nu_1\\le u}s_{(u^d,\\nu)}(-V_+)s_\\nu(-V_-).\n\\]</p><p>Use charge-\\(c\\) wedges with occupied levels \\(c+\\lambda_i-i\\), creation/removal operators \\(\\psi_j,\\psi_j^*\\), and currents\n\\[\nA_k=\\sum_j\\psi_{j-k}\\psi_j^*.\n\\]\nDirect exterior-algebra calculation gives\n\\[\n[A_k,\\psi_j]=\\psi_{j-k},\\quad\n[A_k,\\psi_j^*]=-\\psi_{j+k}^*,\\quad\n[A_k,A_h]=k\\delta_{h,-k}.\n\\]\nThe last commutator is scalar and is evaluated on the vacancy by its \\(k\\) upward moves.</p><p>With \\(\\Gamma_\\pm=\\exp(\\sum_{k&gt;0}t_kA_{\\pm k})\\), the transposed sum is\n\\[\n\\langle d|\\Gamma_+(-V_+)\n\\psi_{u+d-1}\\cdots\\psi_u\n\\prod_{j\\ge u+d}(1-\\psi_j\\psi_j^*)\n\\Gamma_-(-V_-)|0\\rangle.\n\\]\nExpand projectors. Use currents\n\\(\\psi(w)=\\sum_k\\psi_kw^k\\),\n\\(\\psi^*(v)=\\sum_k\\psi_k^*v^{-k}\\).\nCommuting the \\(\\Gamma\\)'s supplies the stated exponentials and scalar prefactor.</p><p>For \\(r=d+l\\), the vacancy expectation is a Cauchy–Vandermonde determinant with columns\n\\[\nw_i^a\\ (0\\le a&lt;d),\\qquad v_j/(w_i-v_j).\n\\]\nSymmetrized level extractions give another determinant with columns\n\\[\nw_i^{-a},\\qquad (v_j/w_i)^d/(1-v_j/w_i),\n\\]\nbesides the powers \\(Y_h^{-u\\tau_h}\\) and inverse factorials. Sum distinct projection levels by minors on separated radii. Polynomial-column subtraction makes the determinant product exactly the stated \\(E\\)-factors, up to signs depending only on counts. All these signs are independent of \\(u\\).</p><p>The level sums converge on separated radii. Hadamard bounds for the two determinants, together with factorials, give absolute convergence for deterministic analytic potentials, uniformly on compact finite-time sets. Formal coefficients agree because finitely many projectors act at fixed weighted degree. Approximate analytic potentials by truncations. Negative \\(d\\) follows by circle inversion and exchanging signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Applying Lemma 9.3 in each color and averaging the Gaussian factors gives per mode \\( \\det A(v)^{-1} \\exp\\!\\left( \\frac{(B^–c^-)^tA(v)^{-1}(B^++c^+)+(c^-)^tc^+}{m} \\right). \\) The interchange of Gaussian averaging and …", "statement": "<p>Hubbard averaging and analytic interchange.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">At mode \\(m&gt;0\\), put \\(v=m\\varepsilon\\) and\n  \\[\n  B^\\pm=nH1+\\sum_iJ_i^\\pm y_i^{\\pm m}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Linearize the upper quadratic form by independent standard circular complex Gaussian columns:\n  \\[\n  V_{\\pm m}=-B^\\mp/m+\\sqrt{1-A}\\,\\xi_{\\pm,m}/\\sqrt m,\n  \\qquad \\xi_{+,m}=\\overline{\\xi_{-,m}}.\n  \\]\n  Take the square root in the fixed real color-eigenbasis.</p><p class=\"li\">For holes \\(Y_h=e^{\\varepsilon h_h}y_h\\), set\n  \\(c^\\pm=\\sum_h\\tau_hY_h^{\\pm m}\\), by color.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Applying Lemma 9.3 in each color and averaging the Gaussian factors gives per mode\n\\[\n\\det A(v)^{-1}\n\\exp\\!\\left(\n\\frac{(B^--c^-)^tA(v)^{-1}(B^++c^+)+(c^-)^tc^+}{m}\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe interchange of Gaussian averaging and hole expansion is absolutely justified at fixed real \\(L\\), lower data, and theta indices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Gaussian integration gives the displayed formula. Including Cauchy pair factors, the hole-hole oriented Fourier kernel is\n\\[\n\\tau\\tau'e^{(h'-h)v}\n(A^{-1}+\\operatorname{sgn}(h-h')1).\n\\]\nHole-lower kernels are\n\\[\n\\tau e^{-hv}A^{-1}J_i^+,\\qquad\n-\\tau(J_i^-)^tA^{-1}e^{hv}.\n\\]\nThe two winding signs combine with lower winding powers to leave no sign depending on \\(M\\).</p><p>Choose tiny separated shifts \\(\\pm h_0\\) inside the random potentials' analytic annulus. After the Szegő prefactor, integrating absolute values leaves, in a color eigenchannel of eigenvalue \\(a\\), the real Hessian\n\\[\ne^{-|h-h'|v}\n-(a^{-1}-1)_+\\sinh(hv)\\sinh(h'v)\n-(1-a^{-1})_+\\cosh(hv)\\cosh(h'v).\n\\]\nIt is positive definite when\n\\[\n\\tanh(h_0v)&lt;a&lt;\\coth(h_0v).\n\\]\nThe explicit eigenvalues have asymptotics \\((9/8)v,1/(2v)\\) at zero and \\(1+O(e^{-v})\\) at infinity, so tiny fixed \\(h_0\\) works.</p><p>At fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\), subtract \\((1-e^{-cv})1\\) for small \\(c&gt;0\\). The remainder stays positive, and missing diagonals cost a constant per hole. Same-shift distinct-point gains are at most \\(c\\varepsilon\\) per pair, absorbed by the favorable Cauchy mean\n\\[\n-2\\varepsilon h_0\\sum_c r_cl_c\n\\]\nbecause count differences are fixed and\n\\(\\binom{r_c}2+\\binom{l_c}2\\le r_cl_c+O(1)\\).\nFactorials now give absolute convergence. Truncate Gaussian modes first; high-mode coefficients decay exponentially, and strict stiffness gives domination and absolute moments slightly above \\(1\\). The bounds are uniform in lower radial positions at fixed preceding data.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The fixed-sector reduction and scalar factorization are given in Lemma 10.1.", "statement": "<p>Residual five-species gas and positive kernel decomposition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Continue from Proposition 9, initially at fixed \\(M\\) and large real \\(L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Move outer holes to offset \\(h=3/8\\), inner holes to \\(h=-1/4\\). The inner-hole crossing with a same-color \\(D\\) creates a bound object \\(P=(D,I)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Free \\(D\\) has offset \\(d=1/8\\), bound \\(P\\) has \\(d=h=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In each color of sign \\(q_c\\), use the five species\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n  \\text{species}&amp;a_i&amp;p_i/q_c&amp;j_i&amp;\\xi_i\\\\ \\hline\n  L&amp;1/2&amp;1&amp;1&amp;0\\\\\n  D&amp;1/8&amp;2&amp;1&amp;1\\\\\n  O&amp;3/8&amp;0&amp;-1&amp;1\\\\\n  I&amp;-1/4&amp;0&amp;1&amp;-1\\\\\n  P&amp;0&amp;2&amp;2&amp;0\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(1=(1,1)^t\\), \\(Q=(1,-1)^t\\). Let \\(\\lambda_i\\) be the color unit column for gas objects and zero for holes, and \\(t_i=\\tau_i\\) times the color unit column for holes and zero for gas. Add constituent data for \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_i=\\sum_{\\nu\\in i}q_\\nu\\theta_\\nu\\), \\(j_i=1^t(\\lambda_i-t_i)\\), and \\(\\xi_i=|p_i|-j_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fixed-sector reduction and scalar factorization are given in Lemma 10.1. Its residual oriented kernel has the positive decomposition established in Lemma 10.2, furnishing both population control and integer-charge variance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 10.1 tracks the contour crossings, residue species, self factors, winding phases, and completion of the count square. Lemma 10.2 gives a positive decomposition of the resulting kernel. No absolute summation over all sectors is used here; it is supplied next.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(M_0=n+O(\\#\\text{objects})\\) is real and independent of positions and periods, \\(\\sum_i j_i=0\\), and \\( G(v)=\\frac{jj^t}{2av}+K_0+O(v),\\qquad G(-v)=-G(v)^t, \\) where …", "statement": "<p>Residual kernel and fixed-sector reduction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Continue \\(J_D^\\pm\\) on their initial branches and define\n  \\[\n  F_i^+=J_i^++t_ie^{h_iv}\n  =T_i/v+f_i+r_iv+\\cdots,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  F_i^-=J_i^--t_ie^{-h_iv}=-F_i^+(-v),\n  \\quad\n  T_i=Qp_i/4,\\quad\n  f_i=Qz_i/4-\\lambda_i+t_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W^g\\) be the direct lower-gas kernel. For an \\(I,D\\) crossing with \\(d&gt;h\\), set\n  \\[\n  C_{ID}=Re^{(d-h)v},\\qquad\n  C_{DI}=-Re^{(h-d)v},\n  \\]\n  where \\(R=1\\) for equal colors and \\(-2\\) otherwise; use half at equality and zero for \\(d&lt;h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  G_{ij}=W^g_{ij}-(F_i^-)^tA^{-1}F_j^+\n  +t_i^tt_j\\operatorname{sgn}(h_i-h_j)e^{(h_j-h_i)v}\n  +C_{ij}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\) be lower virtual counts by color, \\(u=M1-g\\), and impose\n  \\[\n  \\sum_i(t_i-\\lambda_i)=bQ,\\qquad M\\ge\\max_cg_c.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">With \\(P_\\pm\\) the projections onto \\(1,Q\\), put\n  \\[\n  a=9/8,\\quad\n  \\nu_i=-2T_i-P_+r_i/a,\\quad\n  M_01=n1+\\sum_i\\nu_i+g.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(M_0=n+O(\\#\\text{objects})\\) is real and independent of positions and periods, \\(\\sum_i j_i=0\\), and\n\\[\nG(v)=\\frac{jj^t}{2av}+K_0+O(v),\\qquad G(-v)=-G(v)^t,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n(K_0)_{ij}\n=\\frac{p_iz_j-z_ip_j}{4}-k_{ij}\n+\\nu_i^tf_j-f_i^t\\nu_j.\n\\]</p><p>Apart from exact factorial measures, one-object factors bounded by \\(C|dx_i|\\), a count-independent unit-modulus phase, and the \\(\\kappa\\)-free part of \\(B_n\\), each term factors as:\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\pi}{L}\\prod_{m&gt;0}\\det A(m\\varepsilon)^{-1}\n\\exp\\!\\left[n^2\\varepsilon\n\\left(\\tfrac12H_*(0)+\\sum_{m&gt;0}H_*(m\\varepsilon)\\right)\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\exp[-2\\varepsilon(b_1-b_2)^2-a\\varepsilon(M-M_0)^2\n-s(M-n)+i(M-M_0)S],\n\\quad S=\\sum_i j_i\\varphi_i,\n\\]\nan external drive with logarithm\n\\[\n-n\\sum_i\\xi_i\\left[\ni\\varphi_i+a_i\\varepsilon+\n\\sum_{m&gt;0}\\frac{\\rho(m\\varepsilon)}m\n(e^{a_im\\varepsilon}y_i^m-e^{-a_im\\varepsilon}y_i^{-m})\n\\right],\n\\]\nand interaction logarithm\n\\[\n-\\frac\\varepsilon2\\sum_{ij}[v]G_{ij}\n-\\sum_{m&gt;0}\\frac{\n\\sum_{ij}G_{ij}(m\\varepsilon)y_j^my_i^{-m}\n-\\sum_i(1-e^{-m\\varepsilon})}{m}\n-i\\sum_{ij}\\varphi_j(K_0)_{ij},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\rho(v)=\\frac1{2\\cosh v-1},\n\\qquad\nH_*(v)=\\frac{2\\cosh3v-2}{v(2\\cosh v-1)\\sinh4v}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The growing \\(I\\)-to-\\(D\\) part is \\(-Re^{(d-h)v}\\), exponentiating to\n\\((1-e^{(d-h)\\varepsilon}y_D/y_I)^{-R}\\).\nRe-expand it on the opposite Fourier branch, adding \\(C\\), its mean change \\(-\\varepsilon R(d-h)\\), and winding \\(-R\\). Equal-color crossings have a simple pole and bind \\(D,I\\); opposite colors have a zero. Same-color \\(DD,II\\) double zeros compensate further potential poles, so only disjoint matchings occur. Matching factorials become those of residual species and bound pairs.</p><p>Other Fourier factors are analytic through the move: \\(J_D^+\\) has the stated growing column, \\(J_D^-\\) decays at least as \\(e^{-(1+d)v}\\), and \\(J_L^\\pm=O(e^{-v/2})\\). Bare gas top differences stay below \\(1\\). Equal-offset paired factors can use averaged branches together, up to count-dependent signs.</p><p>All final diagonal limits are \\(G_{ii}(\\infty)=1\\). An unbound hole's missing diagonal \\(1\\), changed to \\(1-e^{-v}\\), supplies activity\n\\(\\exp(\\sum e^{-m\\varepsilon}/m)\\) with measure \\(dx_i/L\\), bounded by \\(C|dx_i|\\). For a bound pair, removing the pure pole adds \\(1\\) to its effective diagonal, giving exactly the same final subtraction without extra activity. Lower self factors were bounded in Lemma 9.2.</p><p>Expand\n\\[\nA=P_-/(2v)+A_1v+O(v^3),\\qquad P_+A_1=aP_+.\n\\]\nThe minus-channel pole cancels, giving the displayed \\(G,K_0\\). Direct constants are\n\\((p_iz_j-z_ip_j)/4-k_{ij}\\); dressing adds the \\(\\nu,f\\)-terms.</p><p>For external terms,\n\\[\n-A^{-1}H1=\\rho1,\\qquad\nh_i^+-H1^tA^{-1}F_i^+=\\xi_i\\rho e^{a_iv},\n\\]\nwith reversed term \\(-\\xi_i\\rho e^{-a_iv}\\), and exterior-exterior term\n\\(2H\\rho=-vH_*\\).</p><p>Zero-mode square completion starts from\n\\[\n2|f_{\\rm tot}|^2+4(b_1-b_2)^2+\n[v]\\{u^tAu+2u^t(nH1+\\sum_iF_i^+)+B(v)\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(f_{\\rm tot}=(\\sum_i z_i/4+b)Q\\). Subtracting\n\\[\n(nH1+\\sum_iF_i^-)^tA^{-1}\n(nH1+\\sum_iF_i^+)\n\\]\nleaves \\(4(b_1-b_2)^2+2a(M-M_0)^2\\). The minus-channel completion cancels \\(2|f_{\\rm tot}|^2\\); the plus channel uses \\(P_+f_{\\rm tot}=0\\).</p><p>The winding powers reduce to the displayed count phase and \\(K_0\\)-phase, using\n\\[\n\\sum_i((K_0)_{ij}+k_{ij})\n=bp_j-\\sum_i\\nu_i^t(\\lambda_j-t_j).\n\\]\nUpper winding-determinant signs cancel the \\(M\\)-dependent lower signs. The factor \\(\\kappa^n\\) combines with \\(e^{-sM}\\) to give \\(e^{-s(M-n)}\\). All remaining signs depend only on discrete defect data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(K\\) and sufficiently small \\(c>0\\), subtract:", "statement": "<p>Kernel positivity decomposition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the symmetric part of \\(G(v)\\), indexed by five species in each of two colors, and let \\(j=(1,1,-1,1,2)^t\\) in each color.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(K\\) and sufficiently small \\(c&gt;0\\), subtract:\n\\[\n\\frac{jj^t\\operatorname{sech}^2(Kv)}{2av}\n\\]\non all colors,\n\\[\nR(v)=\\tanh(v/2)\n\\left(\\frac{R_1}{\\cosh v}+\\frac{R_2}{\\cosh(v/2)}\\right)\n+2\\tanh v\\,e_5e_5^t\n\\]\non opposite-color blocks, and \\(\\tanh(cv)\\) on the diagonal, where\n\\[\n8R_1=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n6&amp;15&amp;0&amp;3&amp;16\\\\\n15&amp;22&amp;0&amp;9&amp;26\\\\\n0&amp;0&amp;2&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\n3&amp;9&amp;0&amp;0&amp;5\\\\\n16&amp;26&amp;0&amp;5&amp;5\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n8R_2=2(e_2e_5^t+e_5e_2^t)+5(e_4e_5^t+e_5e_4^t).\n\\]\nThe remainder divided by \\(v\\) is even, smooth, exponentially decreasing, and positive as a matrix Fourier form; it dominates \\(c_1\\operatorname{sech}(c_2v)1\\). The subtracted repulsions divided by \\(v\\) have entrywise nonnegative Fourier transforms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in color channel \\(h=\\pm1\\), and subtract \\(hR\\) first, calling the symmetric result \\(S_h\\). Put\n\\[\nx=e^{v/8},\\quad w=x^8,\\quad D=w^4-w^{-4},\n\\quad A_n=w^{-4}(w^2-w+1)(w^3-h)^2.\n\\]\nAfter removing offset factor \\(x^{8(a_j-a_i)}\\), the oriented entry is\n\\[\nm_{ij}(w)/D-\\frac{p_i(w^{-1})p_j(w)}{DA_n}+b_{ij}.\n\\]\nAt \\(w^8=1\\), the numerator cancels because\n\\(m_{ij}A_n=p_i(w^{-1})p_j(w)\\); at \\(w=1,h=1\\) it vanishes to order at least two.</p><p>With \\(z=w^{-1}\\), set\n\\[\nN_+=44,\\quad N_-=40,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta_+=(1-z)(1+z)^2(1+z^2)(1+z+z^2)^2(1-z+z^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta_-=(1+z)^2(1+z^2)(1-z+z^2)^3.\n\\]\nThen \\(4x^{N_h}\\Delta_hS_h\\) is Laurent polynomial of parity \\(h\\) under \\(x\\mapsto1/x\\), with maximal power \\(N_h\\). Let \\(P_k\\) be its nonnegative-power coefficients, taking half the constant, and\n\\[\nB_p=\\sum_{k=0}^{N_h}(k/N_h)^pP_k.\n\\]\nThe plus channel has \\(B_0=128jj^t\\). Every remaining relevant \\(B_p\\), even \\(p&gt;0\\) in plus and odd \\(p\\) in minus, is positive definite.</p><p>The finite coefficient check is as follows. Use\n\\[\nK_i=(2),(2,3),\\varnothing,\\varnothing,(2,3),\\qquad\n\\tau_i=0,0,1,-1,-1,\n\\]\n\\[\nd_l=(2,-1,1,-2h,2h,-2h,1,-1)_l.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\beta=a_j-a_i\\), define\n\\[\nL_b(m)=\n\\begin{cases}\nz^{8-(m\\bmod8)},&amp;m\\not\\equiv0,\\\\\nz^8,&amp;m\\equiv0,\\ b&gt;0,\\\\\n1,&amp;m\\equiv0,\\ b&lt;0,\\\\\n(1+z^8)/2,&amp;m\\equiv0,\\ b=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nm'_{ij}=z^4m_{ij}\n=\\sum_{r\\in K_i,s\\in K_j,l}d_lL_\\beta(l+s-r),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_j=z^4p_j\n=\\tau_j(1-z^8)+\\sum_{s\\in K_j,l}d_lL_{\\sigma_j}(l+s),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma_j&gt;0\\) on \\(L\\), negative on \\(D,P\\), and\n\\[\nb_{ij}=-\\tau_i\\tau_j\\operatorname{sgn}\\beta\n+(1-2h)\n[1_{\\tau_i=-1,|K_j|=2}\\chi(\\beta)\n-1_{|K_i|=2,\\tau_j=-1}\\chi(-\\beta)].\n\\]</p><p>The integer parts of successive leading minors are:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrr|c|rrrrr}\np_+&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5&amp;p_-&amp;1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5\\\\ \\hline\n2&amp;22&amp;149&amp;542&amp;2294&amp;10192&amp;1&amp;1&amp;11&amp;9&amp;9&amp;3\\\\\n4&amp;10&amp;32&amp;31&amp;54&amp;18&amp;3&amp;1&amp;9&amp;9&amp;11&amp;9\\\\\n6&amp;7&amp;15&amp;28&amp;97&amp;174&amp;5&amp;1&amp;12&amp;14&amp;17&amp;21\\\\\n8&amp;5&amp;13&amp;38&amp;135&amp;211&amp;7&amp;2&amp;15&amp;22&amp;31&amp;59\\\\\n10&amp;4&amp;12&amp;44&amp;150&amp;177&amp;9&amp;2&amp;17&amp;29&amp;43&amp;122\\\\\n12&amp;4&amp;13&amp;47&amp;158&amp;144&amp;11&amp;3&amp;18&amp;33&amp;52&amp;185\\\\\n14&amp;4&amp;13&amp;49&amp;165&amp;125&amp;13&amp;3&amp;17&amp;36&amp;63&amp;238\\\\\n16&amp;4&amp;14&amp;50&amp;173&amp;122&amp;15&amp;3&amp;17&amp;38&amp;77&amp;289\\\\\n18&amp;4&amp;14&amp;52&amp;180&amp;133&amp;17&amp;3&amp;16&amp;40&amp;96&amp;347\\\\\n20&amp;4&amp;14&amp;53&amp;188&amp;156&amp;19&amp;3&amp;16&amp;43&amp;115&amp;412\\\\\n22&amp;4&amp;15&amp;54&amp;196&amp;188&amp;21&amp;3&amp;16&amp;45&amp;135&amp;482\\\\\n24&amp;4&amp;15&amp;56&amp;203&amp;227&amp;23&amp;3&amp;16&amp;48&amp;153&amp;550\\\\\n26&amp;4&amp;15&amp;56&amp;209&amp;271&amp;25&amp;3&amp;15&amp;50&amp;169&amp;614\\\\\n28&amp;4&amp;15&amp;57&amp;215&amp;318&amp;27&amp;3&amp;15&amp;52&amp;183&amp;672\\\\\n30&amp;4&amp;15&amp;58&amp;220&amp;366&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\\\\n32&amp;4&amp;15&amp;59&amp;225&amp;414&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\\\\n34&amp;4&amp;15&amp;59&amp;228&amp;461&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\\\\n36&amp;4&amp;15&amp;60&amp;232&amp;506&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\\\\n38&amp;4&amp;15&amp;60&amp;235&amp;549&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\\\\n40&amp;4&amp;15&amp;60&amp;237&amp;589&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAt \\(p_+=42\\), the integer parts of\n\\(100\\sum_{k&lt;N}\\sum_j(k/N)^p|P_{k,ij}|\\)\nare \\((178,295,150,177,382)\\); at \\(p_-=29\\) they are\n\\((261,374,287,271,231)\\). Since \\(P_N=4I\\), these give strict diagonal dominance, which persists at larger \\(p\\).</p><p>For reproducing the coefficients, form through order six\n\\[\nH_{ij}=\\Delta_h(m'_{ij}+b_{ij})\n-Q_h(z)z^8u_i(1/z)u_j(z),\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_+=(1+z)^2(1+z^2)\\sum_{d=0}^6z^d,\\qquad Q_-=1+z^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{l,ij}\n={}&amp;2[z^{l/8+a_j-a_i}]H_{ij}\\\\\n&amp;-2h[z^{l/8}]\\Delta_h\n\\left(\n2\\frac{1-z}{1+z}\n\\left(\\frac{zR_1}{1+z^2}+\\frac{\\sqrt zR_2}{1+z}\\right)\n+2\\frac{1-z^2}{1+z^2}e_5e_5^t\n\\right)_{ij}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nMissing powers give zero. Use \\(D_l+D_l^t\\), \\(0\\le l&lt;N\\), and the weighted determinants divided by \\(N^{pb}\\). Thus the check uses only the displayed finite polynomial operations.</p><p>Expand in \\(\\log x&gt;0\\):\n\\[\n4x^{44}\\Delta_+S_+\n=2\\sum_{r\\ge0}\\frac{(44\\log x)^{2r}}{(2r)!}B_{2r},\n\\]\n\\[\n4x^{40}\\Delta_-S_-\n=2\\sum_{r\\ge0}\\frac{(40\\log x)^{2r+1}}{(2r+1)!}B_{2r+1}.\n\\]\nAll scalar coefficients are nonnegative. This proves positivity. \\(B_1\\) gives positive \\(S_-/v\\) at zero; \\(B_2\\) gives positive regular coefficient off the plus pole. Taking \\(K\\) large improves the pole-direction coefficient after the smoothed-pole subtraction, while making it arbitrarily small away from zero. At infinity the result is \\(I+O(e^{-c_3v})\\). Small enough \\(c\\) permits diagonal \\(\\tanh(cv)\\) subtraction and the stated positive minorant.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\tanh(cv)/v=\\int_0^c\\operatorname{sech}^2(uv)\\,du.\n\\]\nThe Fourier transform of \\(\\operatorname{sech}\\) is positive; products give positive convolutions. The displayed repulsion factors therefore have nonnegative transforms.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The transformed particle expansions are absolutely summable and admit the complex-period continuation and count summation below.", "statement": "<p>Uniform complex-twist upper estimate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) of Proposition 9 and the residual gas of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\) range in a fixed compact subset of \\(|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Take large complex \\(L\\), with \\(R=\\Re L\\) and bounded \\(|\\Im L|\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The transformed particle expansions are absolutely summable and admit the complex-period continuation and count summation below. After comparing their explicit bulk factors with Proposition 8,\n\\[\n|X_{n,n}(e^s)|\n\\le Cn^{1/6+2\\Re(s^2)/(3\\pi^2)}\n\\]\nlocally uniformly in \\(|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 11.1–11.4 bound and sum the residual gas, retaining only pressure, determinant, and harmonic twist. Lemma 11.5 calibrates the bulk and recovers the unperiodized numerator.</p><p>The \\(\\kappa\\)-free part of \\(B_n\\), multiplied by \\(e^{n^2I}\\), is exactly\n\\[\nD_0^{-2n}(4D_0^2/9)^{2n^2},\n\\]\nthe squared exponential denominator factor in Proposition 8. Remaining factors are bounded by\n\\[\n\\left|\\exp(L/4+s^2/(4a\\varepsilon))\\right|\n\\le Cn^{3/8+2\\Re(s^2)/(3\\pi^2)}.\n\\]\nDivide by the squared denominator estimate and use\n\\[\n\\frac38-2\\cdot\\frac5{48}=\\frac16.\n\\]\nThis proves the complex upper estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The real logarithm of the remaining interaction, including its antisymmetric linear phase, is bounded above by \\( -c\\sum_dn_d^2+Cm. \\) The bound is uniform for the stated complex periods and contours.", "statement": "<p>Population control on gently bent contours.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Positions lie on a common graph\n  \\[\n  \\gamma(r)=r+i\\chi(r),\\quad -R/2&lt;r&lt;R/2,\n  \\qquad \\gamma(r+R)=\\gamma(r)+L,\n  \\]\n  with \\(\\chi\\) odd and bounded, piecewise smooth, and Lipschitz constant \\(\\sigma\\) sufficiently small.</p><p class=\"li\">Divide the real parameter period into boxes of widths between \\(1,2\\), with populations \\(n_d\\), and let \\(m=\\sum_dn_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Remove the smoothed pole term of Lemma 10.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The real logarithm of the remaining interaction, including its antisymmetric linear phase, is bounded above by\n\\[\n-c\\sum_dn_d^2+Cm.\n\\]\nThe bound is uniform for the stated complex periods and contours.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the remaining symmetric numerator divided by \\(v\\), call it \\(f\\), the interaction including zero mode is\n\\[\n-\\frac12\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\n\\widehat f_{ij}((x_j-x_i+kL)/\\alpha).\n\\]\nPoisson summation applies first on the real line and then by continuation. The regular functions are even, analytic in a thin strip, and exponentially decreasing on small tilted rays. Repulsion functions may decrease only as \\(1/|v|\\); their transforms are locally integrable with bounds \\(O(1+|\\log|t||)\\), derivatives \\(O(1+1/|t|)\\), and exponential decay away from zero. These follow by deforming to\n\\(v=u+i(\\delta+2\\sigma_0|u|)\\), with the reflected contour at the other end.</p><p>On the real comparison period, subtract the minorant\n\\(c_1\\operatorname{sech}(c_2v)I\\). The remainder has nonnegative ordered Fourier energy, including diagonals and zero mode. The retained scalar transform is nonnegative everywhere and bounded below for pairs within a box. With ten species,\n\\[\nc\\sum_{d,t}n_{d,t}^2\\ge(c/10)\\sum_dn_d^2.\n\\]\nSeparate repulsions are nonnegative in space; diagonal self corrections cost \\(O(m)\\).</p><p>Changing each lifted real difference by at most \\(\\sigma\\) times its size costs \\(C\\sigma\\sum_dn_d^2\\), by transform derivative bounds and boxwise Cauchy–Schwarz. Small slopes absorb it.</p><p>For \\(K=(G-G^t)/2\\), include the \\(K_0\\)-linear phase. Its pair contribution is the primitive, zero at zero, of\n\\[\n-\\frac{i}{2\\alpha}\\sum_k\\widehat K((x+kL)/\\alpha).\n\\]\nIt is imaginary on the real comparison. Comparing primitives image by image gives the same \\(C\\sigma\\sum_dn_d^2\\) loss; nonzero-period endpoint corrections are exponentially small. This proves the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\bar w_*=-S/(2\\pi), \\) and the nonzero smoothed-pole log is exactly \\( -c_0\\int_\\gamma(w_*-\\bar w_*)^2\\,dz, \\qquad c_0=\\frac{\\pi^2}{2\\pi\\alpha a}. \\) Moreover …", "statement": "<p>Integer-charge variance and joint contour deformation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w(r)\\) be the periodic step function starting at zero at \\(-R/2\\), with jumps \\(j_i\\) at positions \\(r_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Smooth on the complex contour with kernel\n  \\[\n  H(z)=\\frac1{2\\pi\\alpha}\\sum_k\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{sech}(K\\cdot)}((z+kL)/\\alpha),\n  \\]\n  using complex differential \\(dz\\). Denote the result by \\(w_*\\), with average \\(\\bar w_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_R\\) be the analogous smoothing on the real period and \\(w_R^0\\) its average.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  b(r)=\\sum_{i,k}e^{-c|r-r_i+kR|}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\bar w_*=-S/(2\\pi),\n\\]\nand the nonzero smoothed-pole log is exactly\n\\[\n-c_0\\int_\\gamma(w_*-\\bar w_*)^2\\,dz,\n\\qquad c_0=\\frac{\\pi^2}{2\\pi\\alpha a}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n|w_R-w|\\le Cb,\\quad\n|w_*(\\gamma(r))-w_R(r)|\\le C\\sigma b,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int b\\le Cm,\\quad \\int b^2\\le C\\sum_dn_d^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\bar w_*-w_R^0|\n\\le C\\sigma R^{-1/2}\n(\\|w_R-w_R^0\\|_2+\\|b\\|_2).\n\\]\nThe full integrands may be jointly deformed along the specified product tori.</p>", "proof": "<p>On straight contours, integration by parts gives the step's nonzero Fourier coefficients\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sum_i j_ie^{-ib\\varphi_i}}{2\\pi ib},\n\\]\nand smoothing multiplies them by \\(\\operatorname{sech}(Kb\\varepsilon)\\). Fourier series give the variance identity and average.</p><p>For bent contours, split step integrals at moving jump positions. Convolutions become iterated analytic-kernel integrals with endpoints in common analytic neighborhoods. Cauchy deformation continues the identities as positions and arcs move together.</p><p>At coincidences, separate the high-frequency constant from the exponentially decreasing remainder. The constant exponentiates to a nonnegative integer power of\n\\((1-y_j/y_i)(1-y_i/y_j)\\); the remainder converges normally. Thus the full exponential extends through contact loci. Integer winding powers give periodicity at seams for each integer original \\(M\\), so there are no vertical-end terms. These observations justify joint rather than separated-logarithm deformation.</p><p>Both smoothing kernels integrate to one. Convolve differences from the step value at \\(r\\), bounded by jumps passed, to get the first two pointwise estimates. Exponentially decaying local-density sums give the integral bounds. Averaging unsmoothed steps with the perturbed differential gives the average bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The real log of the external drive is at most \\( Cm-c'\\sum_{i:\\xi_i\\ne0} \\exp(4\\operatorname{dist}(r_i,\\mathrm{edge})/3). \\) Before summing \\(M\\), and omitting common pressure/determinant factors, terms have bounds …", "statement": "<p>Sharp external localization and absolute assembly.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  R=(3/2)\\log n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Bend only within a fixed sufficiently wide neighborhood of the two period edges, with small slope.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain the count restriction \\(M\\ge\\max_cg_c\\) initially.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The real log of the external drive is at most\n\\[\nCm-c'\\sum_{i:\\xi_i\\ne0}\n       \\exp(4\\operatorname{dist}(r_i,\\mathrm{edge})/3).\n\\]\nBefore summing \\(M\\), and omitting common pressure/determinant factors, terms have bounds\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left[\nC_nm+CR-c\\sum_dn_d^2-c'(M-M_0)^2/R\n\\right],\n\\]\nwith \\(C_n\\) constant in the sharp setting. These bounds justify absolute assembly, continuation to complex periods, and extension of the count sum to all integers with negligible error.</p>", "proof": "<p>The external bracket is a primitive vanishing at zero with derivative\n\\[\n(i/\\alpha)\\sum_k\\widehat\\rho((x+kL)/\\alpha),\n\\]\nevaluated at \\(x=x_i-i\\alpha a_i\\). Its transform is a positive constant times\n\\[\n\\sinh(2\\pi t/3)/\\sinh\\pi t,\n\\]\nby a \\(2\\pi i\\) shift and residues. It decays as \\(e^{-\\pi|\\Re t|/3}\\), with comparable positive real part for \\(|\\Im t|\\le3/8\\).</p><p>Only \\(D,O,I\\) are driven, and \\(\\xi_i a_i&gt;0\\). The main-line primitive is imaginary; vertical displacement gives signed real part at least \\(ce^{-4|r_i|/3}\\). Other images cost \\(O(e^{-4(R-|r_i|)/3})\\), and edge bends change the primitive by \\(O(1/n)\\). Since \\(ne^{-2R/3}=1\\), this gives the sharp drive bound.</p><p>Use Lemmas 11.1–11.2. The real variance is bounded below by a constant times\n\\(\\|w_R-w_R^0\\|_2^2-O(\\sigma^2\\|b\\|_2^2)\\).\nThe imaginary part of \\(S\\), multiplied by \\(|M-M_0|\\), is absorbed by fractions of count Gaussian, variance, and box-square costs. Complete the real-tilt square using \\(M_0=n+O(m)\\).</p><p>Factorial measures and the displayed population/count bounds give absolute convergence. Thus fixed-sector termwise identities become assembled identities. Straight-contour expressions are holomorphic for large complex periods, with local uniform convergence by nonsharp bounds; this continues each sector, including zero sectors \\(M&gt;2n\\). Joint bending is justified by Lemma 11.2.</p><p>Extending \\(M\\) to all integers uses count-independent factors. If \\(m\\) is a small fraction of \\(n\\), the omitted tail costs \\(cn^2/R\\); otherwise retain box cost \\(cm^2/R\\). Unlocalized factorial sums with \\(e^{Cm}\\) are \\(\\exp(O(R))\\), so either error is negligible even relative to \\(\\exp(-CR)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After extracting \\( \\exp(s^2/(4a\\varepsilon)), \\) the total defect sum is \\(O(1)\\), uniformly in \\(n\\), large \\(\\Re L\\), and bounded \\(\\Im L\\).", "statement": "<p>Poisson count summation and bounded defect sum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the sharp setting of Lemma 11.3 and \\(s\\) in a compact subset of \\(|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After extracting\n\\[\n\\exp(s^2/(4a\\varepsilon)),\n\\]\nthe total defect sum is \\(O(1)\\), uniformly in \\(n\\), large \\(\\Re L\\), and bounded \\(\\Im L\\). The Gaussian sum over \\(b_1-b_2\\), the Poisson prefactor, and the original \\(2\\pi/L\\) together contribute only a bounded factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Poisson summation in \\(M\\) gives\n\\[\n(\\pi/(a\\varepsilon))^{1/2}e^{-s(M_0-n)}\n\\]\nand an integer sum over \\(l\\), with phases of modulus one. Combining its exponent with pole variance gives\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left[-c_0\\int_\\gamma\n(w_*-l-is/(2\\pi))^2\\,dz\\right].\n\\]\nExtract the stated harmonic-twist factor.</p><p>The remaining suppression is at least\n\\[\nc\\|w_R-l\\|_2^2-Cm-C\\sigma^2\\|b\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nIndeed, after replacing complex smoothing by real smoothing, the square's real linear coefficient is\n\\[\nt=(\\Im s+\\chi'\\Re s)/\\pi,\n\\]\nstrictly within \\((-1,1)\\) for small enough slopes. For such \\(t\\),\n\\[\nx^2+tx\\ge c'x^2-C\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nThe unsmoothed step is integer-valued, its smoothing error is \\(Cb\\), and quadratic errors are absorbed by box costs.</p><p>Put\n\\[\ng_0(r)=2\\log(2+\\operatorname{dist}(r,\\mathrm{edge})).\n\\]\nIntegration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i j_i g_0(r_i)\n=-\\int(w-l)g_0'\\,dr\n\\le\\tfrac12c\\|w_R-l\\|_2^2+C+Cm.\n\\]\nOnly \\(O\\) has negative \\(j\\); its external cost absorbs the wrong sign. Retain \\(e^{-g_0(r_i)}\\) on every object, each with bounded integral. Factorials and bounded activities give an \\(O(1)\\) total sum, and unused variance sums boundedly over \\(l\\).</p><p>The \\(b_1-b_2\\) Gaussian costs \\(O(\\sqrt R)\\); with the Poisson factor it is canceled in order by \\(2\\pi/L\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an absolute \\(c_*\\), \\( -\\sum_{m>0}\\log\\det A(m\\varepsilon)=L/4+O(1), \\) \\( \\varepsilon\\left(\\tfrac12H_*(0)+\\sum_{m>0}H_*(m\\varepsilon)\\right) =I+c_*e^{-L}+O(e^{-4\\Re L/3}), \\) where …", "statement": "<p>Bulk calibration and recovery at zero nome.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(H_*\\) and \\(A\\) from Proposition 10, and \\(D_0=2+\\sqrt2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute \\(c_*\\),\n\\[\n-\\sum_{m&gt;0}\\log\\det A(m\\varepsilon)=L/4+O(1),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\varepsilon\\left(\\tfrac12H_*(0)+\\sum_{m&gt;0}H_*(m\\varepsilon)\\right)\n=I+c_*e^{-L}+O(e^{-4\\Re L/3}),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nI=\\int_0^\\infty H_*(v)\\,dv,\\qquad e^I=8D_0^3/81.\n\\]\nOn \\(|q_{\\rm rad}|=n^{-3/4}\\), multiplying \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) by\n\\(e^{-c_*n^2q_{\\rm rad}^2}\\) leaves bounded pressure error and permits Cauchy recovery at zero nome.</p>", "proof": "<p>The eigenvalues of \\(A\\) are\n\\[\n(2\\cosh v-1)(\\cosh3v\\mp1)/\\sinh4v.\n\\]\nThe negative log determinant is smooth at zero and exponentially decreasing. Its positive-line integral is \\(\\pi^2/8\\), obtained by integrating\n\\[\n2[\\log(1-e^{-8v})-\\log(1-e^{-6v})\n-\\log(1-e^{-v}+e^{-2v})].\n\\]\nEuler summation, also on slightly rotated rays, gives \\(L/4+O(1)\\).</p><p>Poisson summation of the even extension of \\(H_*\\), shifting the transform contour through the first two possible poles \\(i\\pi/4,i\\pi/3\\), gives the pressure expansion.</p><p>For \\(y=e^{-v}\\),\n\\[\nvH_*(v)\n=\\frac{2y^2(1-y^3)^2}{(1-y+y^2)(1-y^8)}\n=\\frac{P(y)}{1-y^{24}}.\n\\]\nThe coefficients satisfy \\(P_j=P_{24-j}\\), sum zero, and, below \\(12\\), are\n\\[\nP_2=P_3=2,\\quad P_5=P_6=-6,\\quad\nP_8=P_9=8,\\quad P_{10}=2,\\quad P_{11}=-6.\n\\]\nGroupwise integration against \\(dv/v\\), using the double zero of \\(P\\), gives\n\\[\nI=-\\sum_{j&lt;12}P_j\\log\\sin(\\pi j/24).\n\\]\nThe symmetric sine product follows from\n\\[\n\\prod_{l\\ge0}\\frac{(l+x)(l+1-x)}{(l+1/2)^2}\n=\\sin\\pi x.\n\\]\nThe sine pairs at \\(2,10\\), \\(5,11\\), and \\(3,9\\) have products\n\\(1/4,(1+\\sqrt2)/4,\\sqrt2/4\\), giving \\(e^I\\).</p><p>At \\(\\Re L=(3/2)\\log n\\), the remaining error\n\\(n^2e^{-4\\Re L/3}\\) is \\(O(1)\\). The removed analytic term itself is of order \\(\\sqrt n\\), so is retained explicitly. Since its correcting factor equals \\(1\\) at zero,\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_n(0,s)\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_{|q_{\\rm rad}|=n^{-3/4}}\ne^{-c_*n^2q_{\\rm rad}^2}\n\\mathcal N_n(q_{\\rm rad},s)\\,\\frac{dq_{\\rm rad}}{q_{\\rm rad}}.\n\\]\nThe bounds hold around the full circle.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The \\(E_n\\) are locally uniformly bounded in \\(|\\Im s|<\\pi\\) and satisfy \\(E_n(i\\pi/2)=1\\).", "statement": "<p>Analytic normalization consequences.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For homogeneous balanced contractions, define\n\\[\nE_n(s)=X_{n,n}(e^s)\n\\exp[-(1/6+2s^2/(3\\pi^2))\\log n].\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The \\(E_n\\) are locally uniformly bounded in \\(|\\Im s|&lt;\\pi\\) and satisfy\n\\(E_n(i\\pi/2)=1\\). Consequently:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F_n\\) has a common zero-free disk about \\(\\eta=0\\);</p><p class=\"li\">every fixed order-\\(k\\) Taylor coefficient of \\(\\log F_n\\) there is \\(O_k(1+\\log n)\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 5 and 11 give the normalization and local boundedness of \\(E_n\\). Lemmas 12.1–12.2 give the stated zero-free, coefficient, and positive-fugacity conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a neighborhood of \\(s=i\\pi/2\\) where \\( |E_n(s)-1|<1/2 \\) uniformly in \\(n\\).", "statement": "<p>Uniform zero-free neighborhood and logarithmic coefficients.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 12 and \\(\\eta=2\\cosh s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a neighborhood of \\(s=i\\pi/2\\) where\n\\[\n|E_n(s)-1|&lt;1/2\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(n\\). Its image contains a fixed disk about \\(\\eta=0\\), and on that disk the stated coefficient bounds hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 and Cauchy derivative bounds give a common neighborhood on which the normalized functions remain within \\(1/2\\) of their value \\(1\\). The map \\(2\\cosh s\\) has nonzero derivative at \\(i\\pi/2\\), hence a local analytic inverse. Apply Cauchy estimates to \\(\\log E_n\\), and add the explicit logarithmic normalization, whose coefficients are \\(O_k(\\log n)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( F_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Matching exponent at positive fugacity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 12 and the nonnegative coefficients of \\(F_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nF_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The upper bound is Proposition 11 at \\(s=0\\). From any subsequence choose a further locally uniformly convergent subsequence of \\(E_n\\), by Cauchy bounds and diagonal compact exhaustion. Its holomorphic limit is nonzero at \\(i\\pi/2\\), hence is not identically zero. There are arbitrarily small \\(t&gt;0\\) at which its value at \\(it\\) is nonzero. At such points,\n\\[\nF_n(2)\\ge F_n(2\\cos t)\n\\]\nby coefficient positivity, giving lower exponent\n\\(1/6-2t^2/(3\\pi^2)\\). Let \\(t\\downarrow0\\). This applies to every original subsequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 7.1 (Localization of surrounding polygons)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( Z(R)=R^{1/12+o(1)}. \\) Changing the disk cutoff by a fixed factor does not affect the exponent.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(Z(R)\\) be the fugacity-\\(2\\) partition function of mutually vertex-disjoint planar honeycomb polygons enclosing face center \\(0\\) and lying within distance \\(R\\) of it; include the empty system.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nZ(R)=R^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nChanging the disk cutoff by a fixed factor does not affect the exponent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let physical circumference be \\(p=2n\\). Essential separating polygons form two families exchanged by translation by \\(n\\); opposite families cannot coexist. A finite family of one type of mass \\(B\\), together with its translated copy, has mass \\(2B\\) and incompatibility numerator at least \\(2B^2\\). Lemma 13.1 gives \\(B\\le r_0^{-1}\\). Thus essential single-polygon mass is \\(O(1)\\).</p><p>Contractible polygons lift to disks whose interiors are disjoint from nonzero period translates. In each marked-face family, Lemma 13.3 bounds lifted-radius \\(&gt;p^{1+\\delta}\\) mass by \\(O(1)\\).</p><p>Two independent small disk gases near the two marks give\n\\[\nZ(cp)^2\\le F_n(2)=n^{1/6+o(1)}. \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nRemove essential and oversized polygons. Ignoring their avoidance costs at most\n\\[\n\\prod_{\\rm removed}(1+2w(P))\n\\le\\exp(2\\sum_{\\rm removed}w(P))=O(1).\n\\]\nRetained polygons lift into two radius-\\(p^{1+\\delta}\\) disk systems; dropping inter-system restrictions gives\n\\[\nF_n(2)\\le C_\\delta Z(p^{1+\\delta})^2. \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThus the upper exponent is \\(1/12\\), the lower at least \\(1/(12(1+\\delta))\\). Monotonicity fills radii; let \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( d_{\\mathcal H}\\le r_0^{-1}, \\) independently of circumference and family.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r_0&gt;0\\) be a common zero-free radius of \\(F_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On any balanced cylinder, polygons have weights \\(w(P)=x_*^{|P|}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I(P,Q)\\) indicate incompatibility, including \\(I(P,P)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite nonempty family \\(\\mathcal H\\), put\n  \\[\n  M_{\\mathcal H}=\\sum_{P\\in\\mathcal H}w(P),\n  \\quad\n  d_{\\mathcal H}\n  =\\frac{\\sum_{P,Q\\in\\mathcal H}w(P)I(P,Q)w(Q)}\n         {M_{\\mathcal H}}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nd_{\\mathcal H}\\le r_0^{-1},\n\\]\nindependently of circumference and family.</p>", "proof": "<p>The connected-graph expansion of \\(\\log F_n\\) assigns a tuple \\((P_1,\\ldots,P_k)\\) the alternating connected-spanning-subgraph sum times \\(\\prod_iw(P_i)/k!\\). Repeated polygons still occupy distinct tuple positions. Finite total polygon weight gives coefficientwise convergence.</p><p>Multiplication by \\((-1)^{k-1}\\) makes this alternating sum the number of acyclic orientations with unique source at vertex \\(1\\), by the rooted chromatic-polynomial identity. Thus all tuple contributions have the same sign. A Hamilton path starting at \\(1\\) gives a distinct such orientation, since its directed order is forced. Summing remaining-vertex orders gives\n\\[\n\\left|[\\eta^k]\\log F_n(\\eta)\\right|\n\\ge\\frac1k\n\\sum_{P_1,\\ldots,P_k\\in\\mathcal H}\n\\prod_iw(P_i)\\prod_{i&lt;k}I(P_i,P_{i+1}). \\tag{7.1}\n\\]</p><p>In the weighted inner product\n\\(\\langle f,g\\rangle_w=\\sum_Pw(P)f(P)g(P)\\),\nlet\n\\[\n(Af)(P)=\\sum_QI(P,Q)w(Q)f(Q).\n\\]\nThe right-hand walk sum is \\(\\langle1,A^{k-1}1\\rangle_w\\). For odd \\(k\\), the even spectral-moment inequality gives\n\\[\n\\langle1,A^{k-1}1\\rangle_w\n\\ge M_{\\mathcal H}d_{\\mathcal H}^{k-1}.\n\\]\nFreeze the family and cylinder, and let odd \\(k\\to\\infty\\). Analyticity of \\(\\log F_n\\) in \\(|\\eta|&lt;r_0\\) bounds coefficient root growth by \\(r_0^{-1}\\), proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.3 (Six-rotation radius bound)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( D\\le Cp\\log(D+2) \\) for an absolute constant \\(C\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}\\), \\(\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be the open Jordan interior of a honeycomb polygon \\(Q\\) enclosing \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose an open \\(U\\) contains \\(\\omega^rV\\), \\(0\\le r\\le5\\), and\n  \\[\n  U\\cap(U+kp)=\\varnothing\n  \\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}),\\quad p\\ge1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(D=\\max_{z\\in Q}|z|\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nD\\le Cp\\log(D+2)\n\\]\nfor an absolute constant \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a piecewise-linear \\(\\gamma\\subset V\\) from \\(0\\) to radius \\(D-O(1)\\), with \\(O((D+1)^2)\\) bounded-length segments, following interior faces and then perturbed generically. Except at its start, avoid\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{r=1}^5(1-\\omega^r)^{-1}p\\Lambda.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(S(t)\\) be the six rotated prefixes. Then\n\\[\nS(t)\\cap(S(t)+\\lambda)=\\varnothing\n\\qquad(\\lambda\\in p\\Lambda\\setminus\\{0\\}). \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nOtherwise take first contact. Tip-tip contact is excluded by the avoided fixed-point sets. A tip can meet older points from only one other center. Rotation and translation make the contact graph the full orbit of one displacement \\(l\\), with components the cosets of \\(l\\Lambda\\) in \\(p\\Lambda\\). Distinct components would carry periodic paths in directions \\(l,\\omega l\\), forced to cross in a large parallelogram. Thus \\(l\\Lambda=p\\Lambda\\), so \\(l/p\\) is a unit and the orbit contains horizontal displacement \\(p\\), contradicting period-freeness of \\(U\\).</p><p>The physical quotient\n\\[\n\\mathcal O=\\mathbb C/(p\\Lambda\\rtimes\\langle\\omega\\rangle)\n\\]\nis a sphere with cone marks \\(A,B,C\\) of orders \\(6,3,2\\). Mark the regular projected endpoint \\(q\\). By (7.2), radius is single-valued on the projected path. Split at intersections and erase loops to obtain a simple endpoint arc \\(\\alpha:A\\to q\\) avoiding \\(B,C\\), whose terminal lift still has radius \\(D-O(1)\\).</p><p>Identify the four-marked sphere topologically with the pillowcase\n\\[\n\\mathbb R^2/(2\\mathbb Z^2\\rtimes\\{\\pm1\\}),\n\\]\nwhose branched double cover is \\(\\mathbb R^2/(2\\mathbb Z^2)\\). Use flag-triangle subdivisions and affine triangle identifications. The projected arc has polynomially many bounded pieces in \\(D+1\\), uniformly in \\(q\\), even for thin triangles. Its auxiliary lift ends at an integer \\(u\\), with\n\\[\n|u|\\le(D+2)^{C_0}.\n\\]\nThe doubled arc is a simple torus curve with homology \\(u\\), nonzero by endpoint parity and primitive by a dual intersection-one curve.</p><p>The straight primitive auxiliary arc has the same endpoint corners and slope. To compare it with \\(\\alpha\\), take boundaries of narrow neighborhoods enclosing their endpoint marks. If these boundaries intersect in minimal position, Euler's formula gives\n\\[\n\\sum_F(4-\\#\\text{sides}(F))=8.\n\\]\nNo unmarked bigon is permitted, so there are exactly four marked bigons and otherwise quadrilaterals. In the branched double cover all become quadrilaterals, producing a flat square torus in which the two curve families cross perpendicularly and hence have different slopes, a contradiction. Thus the boundaries, and then the arcs, are isotopic relative to marks.</p><p>This isotopy preserves terminal physical radius. A disk containing only \\(A,q\\) lifts near \\(0\\) with monodromy only rotation about \\(0\\), so all its \\(A\\)-to-\\(q\\) arcs have equal terminal radius; apply this locally throughout the isotopy.</p><p>The Euclidean algorithm sends the unit horizontal vector to \\(u\\) by \\(O(1+\\log|u|)\\) blocks of shear powers \\(H(x,y)=(x+y,y)\\) and quarter turns. Each shear exponent can be reduced modulo \\(12\\) without changing terminal physical radius: \\(H^2\\) is a Dehn twist separating corners into pairs, and the side containing \\(q\\) contains exactly one cone mark, whose monodromy has order dividing \\(6\\). Six twists insert trivial monodromy loops. Conjugated internal block replacements have the same property.</p><p>The shortened word has \\(O(\\log(D+2))\\) factors from a fixed finite collection. Separate its mod-\\(2\\) corner permutation by representatives \\(r_i\\):\n\\[\nh_i=r_{i-1}g_ir_i^{-1},\\qquad\nW=h_1\\cdots h_mr_m.\n\\]\nEach \\(h_i\\) is pure. The bounded-complexity initial representative arc has physical radius \\(O(p)\\).</p><p>Each pure map lifts to a homeomorphism \\(\\widetilde f\\) of the physical plane fixing \\(0\\): it preserves the lifting subgroup normally generated by peripheral powers \\(6,3,2\\) and the regular-mark loop. Its induced action preserves the translation subgroup, the infinite-order deck transformations, so\n\\[\n\\widetilde f(z+\\lambda)=\\widetilde f(z)+L_0\\lambda,\n\\qquad\\lambda\\in p\\Lambda. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nIt intertwines \\(\\omega\\) with \\(\\omega\\) or \\(\\omega^{-1}\\); thus \\(L_0\\) is a similarity and, being a lattice automorphism, orthogonal.</p><p>On one period cell, the fixed finite maps send paths through boundedly many auxiliary/physical triangles, giving \\(|\\widetilde f(z_0)|\\le Cp\\), uniformly in \\(q\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\widetilde f(z)|\\le|z|+C'p.\n\\]\nApply the \\(O(\\log(D+2))\\) pure lifts to the initial arc, obtaining the asserted radius bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Corollary 7.4 (Removing oversized polygons)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(p\\), \\( \\sum_{\\substack{P\\in\\mathcal U\\\\\\max_{z\\in P}|z|>p^{1+\\delta}}} x_*^{|P|}=O(1), \\) uniformly in family and period direction.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). Let \\(\\mathcal U\\) be planar polygons enclosing \\(0\\), each having interior disjoint from nontrivial translates by a common pure lattice period of length \\(p\\). The period may point in any direction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(p\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{P\\in\\mathcal U\\\\\\max_{z\\in P}|z|&gt;p^{1+\\delta}}}\nx_*^{|P|}=O(1),\n\\]\nuniformly in family and period direction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Rotate coordinates for the geometric argument. For any finite oversized subset of mass \\(B\\), place it and all six lattice rotations on a sufficiently large ordinary balanced cylinder, with each enclosing one mark only. Lemma 13.1 is uniform in this containing circumference.</p><p>For an area-ordered pair \\(P,Q\\), if every rotation of \\(Q\\) were compatible with \\(P\\), their disks, all enclosing \\(0\\), would lie strictly inside \\(P\\)'s disk. Its period-free interior would serve as \\(U\\) in Lemma 13.2, giving\n\\(D_Q\\le Cp\\log(D_Q+2)\\), contrary to \\(D_Q&gt;p^{1+\\delta}\\) for large \\(p\\).</p><p>Area-ordered pairs have mass at least \\(B^2/2\\). Assign each an incompatible rotation of \\(Q\\). Each resulting pair has at most six preimages, so the union's incompatibility numerator is at least \\(B^2/12\\), and its mass at most \\(6B\\). Lemma 13.1 gives \\(B\\le72r_0^{-1}\\). Exhaust finite subsets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 8.1 (Finite visit recursion)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\begin{aligned} T_D(v)=3 &+\\sum_{\\substack{P\\subset D\\\\v\\in P}} 2x_*^{|P|-1}[-\\cos(\\sigma(2\\pi-\\tau_v(P)))]\\\\ &+\\sum_{\\substack{P\\subset D\\\\v\\in P^\\circ}} 2x_*^{|P|}T_{P^\\circ}(v). \\end{aligned} \\) All coefficients …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A finite vertex set \\(D\\) is filled if every vertex strictly inside a simple polygon of its induced honeycomb graph belongs to \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Boundary ports cut edges with one endpoint in \\(D\\), one outside.</p><p class=\"li\">Excursions join distinct ordered boundary ports and visit distinct vertices of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v\\in D\\), define\n  \\[\n  T_D(v)=\\sum_{\\gamma\\ni v}x_*^{|\\gamma|}e^{i\\sigma W(\\gamma)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a face \\(f\\) incident to \\(v\\), let \\(Z_D(f)\\) count disjoint polygons in \\(D\\) enclosing \\(f\\), each weighted \\(2x_*^{|P|}\\), including the empty system.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P^\\circ\\) be the strict interior vertex set and\n  \\(\\tau_v(P)\\in\\{\\pi/3,-\\pi/3\\}\\) the counterclockwise polygon turn.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_D(v)=3\n&amp;+\\sum_{\\substack{P\\subset D\\\\v\\in P}}\n2x_*^{|P|-1}[-\\cos(\\sigma(2\\pi-\\tau_v(P)))]\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{\\substack{P\\subset D\\\\v\\in P^\\circ}}\n2x_*^{|P|}T_{P^\\circ}(v). \\qquad(8.1)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll coefficients are positive. Hence \\(T_D(v)\\ge3\\), is monotone under filled-set inclusion, and\n\\[\nc\\sum_{f\\sim v}Z_D(f)\\le T_D(v)\\le C\\sum_{f\\sim v}Z_D(f).\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) is cut out by tiling lines in a convex polygon, and \\(A_D(v)\\) is its positive ordered visit mass, then\n\\[\n\\cos(3\\pi/8)A_D(v)\\le T_D(v)\\le A_D(v).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 14.1–14.2 prove the recursion, positivity, monotonicity, and incident-face comparison. For convex \\(D\\), close each excursion by the counterclockwise boundary arc. Its turn is the lifted change of outward normal minus \\(\\pi\\), hence lies in \\([-\\pi,\\pi]\\). Taking real parts gives the final positive-weight comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The recursion (8.1) holds.", "statement": "<p>Finite exploration identity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the filled domain of Proposition 14. Stop an exploration just before choosing continuation at its first repeated vertex; a first-arrival prefix has not yet taken the arriving vertex's weight or turn.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The recursion (8.1) holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a fresh vertex, continuation weights \\(x_*e^{\\pm i\\sigma\\pi/3}\\) sum to \\(1\\), so replacing an exploration-tree node by its two children preserves total weight.</p><p>Starting from a boundary port, a first repeat consists of an exterior stem and attached polygon. Filling forces the stem outside. Reversing the polygon gives additional turns \\(\\pm4\\pi/3\\), whose phases cancel. This remains true after restricting to histories visiting \\(v\\). Continuing a first-arrival prefix at \\(v\\) has total terminal weight \\(1\\); thus summed prefixes equal \\(T_D(v)\\).</p><p>Now start at \\(v\\) along its three edges, with no weight or turn at \\(v\\), and stop at exit, return to \\(v\\), or repeat elsewhere. Total weight is \\(3\\). Exit terms reverse the counted prefixes, giving \\(T_D(v)\\), real by reversal.</p><p>A return polygon through \\(v\\) contributes\n\\[\n2x_*^{|P|-1}\\cos(\\sigma(2\\pi-\\tau_v(P))).\n\\]\nFor a repeat elsewhere, exterior \\(v\\) gives cancellation. If \\(v\\) lies inside \\(P\\), the stem approaches the inward unused edge, replacing turn \\(-\\pi/3\\) by \\(+\\pi/3\\). Added turns are \\(\\pm8\\pi/3\\), both phase \\(-1\\). Fixed-\\(P\\) stems are exactly prefixes in \\(P^\\circ\\) to its boundary ports and sum to \\(T_{P^\\circ}(v)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n3=T_D(v)+\\sum_{P\\ni v}2x_*^{|P|-1}\n\\cos(\\sigma(2\\pi-\\tau_v(P)))\n-\\sum_{v\\in P^\\circ}2x_*^{|P|}T_{P^\\circ}(v),\n\\]\nwhich rearranges to (8.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Iteration of (8.1) is a positive sum over outer-to-inner disjoint polygon chains strictly enclosing \\(v\\), with final multiplier …", "statement": "<p>Positive nesting expansion and polygon comparison.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 14 and put\n\\[\nc_1=-\\cos(5\\pi/8)&gt;0,\\qquad\nc_2=-\\cos(7\\pi/8)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Iteration of (8.1) is a positive sum over outer-to-inner disjoint polygon chains strictly enclosing \\(v\\), with final multiplier\n\\[\n3+\\frac2{x_*}\\sum_{\\substack{Q\\subset D'\\\\v\\in Q}}\nc_{m_Q}x_*^{|Q|},\n\\qquad m_Q\\in\\{1,2\\}.\n\\]\nThe corresponding expansion of \\(\\sum_{f\\sim v}Z_D(f)\\) has final multiplier\n\\[\n3+2\\sum_{\\substack{Q\\subset D'\\\\v\\in Q}}m_Qx_*^{|Q|}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A through-polygon enclosing one incident face has turn \\(+\\pi/3\\), and one enclosing two has turn \\(-\\pi/3\\), giving \\(c_1,c_2\\). Iteration terminates because strict interiors have fewer vertices. This proves positivity and filled-set monotonicity.</p><p>A strict enclosing polygon encloses all three incident face centers. A through-polygon encloses exactly \\(m_Q\\) of them. Conversely, a polygon enclosing an incident face has \\(v\\) inside or on its boundary. A disjoint system has at most one through-polygon, necessarily innermost. Thus the two expansions have the same strict chains and displayed final multipliers. The fixed ratios \\(c_m/(x_*m)\\) give the comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The tail and first-length bounds of Lemmas 15.1–15.2 hold.", "statement": "<p>Exponential strip tails and lateral exhaustions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Consider fixed-source critical exits in an \\(h\\)-layer strip or convex tiling-line truncation. Let \\(X(\\gamma)\\) be maximum horizontal distance of visited vertices from the source.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The tail and first-length bounds of Lemmas 15.1–15.2 hold. In particular all fixed-\\(h\\) exit length sums are finite, and discarding \\(X\\ge h\\log^2h\\) costs less than every fixed inverse power of \\(h\\), both for mass and first-length mass.</p>", "proof": "<p>The two lemmas give finiteness and all asserted exhaustion controls. At \\(t=h\\log^2h\\), the exponential factor dominates every fixed power of \\(h^{-1}\\), even with the polynomial first-length factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2 (Lateral tail), mass assertion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For absolute \\(C,c>0\\), \\( \\sum_{X(\\gamma)\\ge t}x_*^{|\\gamma|} \\le Ce^{-ct/(h+1)} \\qquad(t\\ge0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 15.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For absolute \\(C,c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{X(\\gamma)\\ge t}x_*^{|\\gamma|}\n\\le Ce^{-ct/(h+1)}\n\\qquad(t\\ge0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the right tail, impose cuts with outward normals\n\\((\\sqrt3/2,1/2)\\), \\((\\sqrt3/2,-1/2)\\).\nLet \\(E_+,E_-\\) be old exits lost under these cuts. Flux subtraction puts their phased sums on distinct rays\n\\(e^{-i\\sigma\\pi/3}\\), \\(e^{-2i\\sigma\\pi/3}\\).\nStagger intercepts by \\(O(h+1)\\) and round to tiling grids so that\n\\[\n\\{X_+&gt;r+C(h+1)\\}\\subset E_-\\subset E_+\\subset\\{X_+&gt;r\\}.\n\\]\nConvexity makes all old real weights comparable to positive weights. Ray separation forces \\(E_+\\setminus E_-\\) to carry a fixed fraction of \\(E_-\\), yielding fixed-factor decay per \\(C(h+1)\\). Reflect for the left tail.</p><p>An exit is lost exactly when it visits a removed vertex; a newly clipped exit attempts one across the new cut. Old valid exits cancel even when their port belongs to both boundaries. Thus nesting introduces no missing boundary term. Constants are uniform in truncation; monotone exhaustion gives the strip.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\sum_{X(\\gamma)\\ge t}|\\gamma|x_*^{|\\gamma|} \\le C(h+1)(h+t+1)e^{-ct/(h+1)}. \\) For each side source, total length mass is bounded at fixed \\(h\\), uniformly in truncation width.", "statement": "<p>Length insertion and remote side sources.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 15. For side sources in a convex lateral truncation, measure displacement toward the far part of the strip.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{X(\\gamma)\\ge t}|\\gamma|x_*^{|\\gamma|}\n\\le C(h+1)(h+t+1)e^{-ct/(h+1)}.\n\\]\nFor each side source, total length mass is bounded at fixed \\(h\\), uniformly in truncation width. Contributions from distant side sources to a fixed marked vertex vanish as width tends to infinity.</p>", "proof": "<p>A simple path in \\(h\\) layers and horizontal range \\(X\\) has at most\n\\(C(h+1)(X+1)\\) vertices. Integrate the exponential mass tail.</p><p>For side sources, use the two slanted cuts facing the far part. Their distinct angles from the initial inward direction lie strictly between \\(-\\pi,\\pi\\), so the same separated-ray proof gives \\(Ce^{-ct/(h+1)}\\). Displacement toward the near end is \\(O(h+1)\\), and the same geometric length bound applies. A fixed marked vertex lies at lateral distance tending to infinity from side sources, giving vanishing mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal M_R=R^{3/4+o(1)}. \\) Uniformly in the central third, \\( V_R(v)=R^{1/12+o(1)}, \\) and \\(V_R(v)\\le R^{1/12+o(1)}\\) uniformly everywhere.", "statement": "<p>Macroscopic hexagon normalizer and visit numerator.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the hexagon and ordered macroscopic excursions of Theorem 2. Let \\(\\mathcal M_R\\) be their total mass, and \\(V_R(v)\\) their unnormalized positive mass through \\(v\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal M_R=R^{3/4+o(1)}.\n\\]\nUniformly in the central third,\n\\[\nV_R(v)=R^{1/12+o(1)},\n\\]\nand \\(V_R(v)\\le R^{1/12+o(1)}\\) uniformly everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Combine Lemmas 16.1–16.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\mathcal M_R=R^{3/4+o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<p>Hexagon normalizing mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 16.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\mathcal M_R=R^{3/4+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a lower bound, choose sources in the central third of one side. Every noninitial-side exit is macroscopic. Flux and comparison of bottom returns with arches in a containing \\(O(R)\\)-layer strip give non-bottom mass at least \\(b_{O(R)}\\) per source. Proposition 6 and order \\(R\\) sources give the lower exponent.</p><p>For an upper bound per source, impose the parallel cut at height\n\\(h=\\lfloor R/\\log^2R\\rfloor\\). Flux subtraction bounds removed exits by \\(Cb_h\\), since new prefixes are strip bridges. A retained macroscopic path reaches lateral distance comparable to \\(R\\), and Proposition 15 bounds it by \\(Ce^{-c\\log^2R}\\). Sum over \\(O(R)\\) sources.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The stated bulk equality and global upper bound for \\(V_R(v)\\) hold.", "statement": "<p>Hexagon visit numerator.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 16.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The stated bulk equality and global upper bound for \\(V_R(v)\\) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For central-third vertices every visiting excursion is macroscopic. Proposition 14 compares visit mass to surrounding-polygon sums. Inscribed and containing disks of comparable radius, with Proposition 13, give exponent \\(1/12\\). At arbitrary vertices, the containing-disk comparison gives the same upper exponent. Macroscopic restriction only decreases the positive mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The uniform visit estimates of Lemma 17.1 hold.", "statement": "<p>Strip visit weights and mixed first-length mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_{S_h}(v)\\) be positive ordered strip excursion mass through \\(v\\), with both endpoints free on the two horizontal boundaries.</p><p class=\"li\">In a convex lateral truncation \\(D\\), allow all its boundary ports and write \\(A_D(v)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\vartheta&lt;1/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The uniform visit estimates of Lemma 17.1 hold. Averaging them gives fixed-source first-length mass \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\) under the reach-\\(\\vartheta h\\) condition and an upper bound \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\) without it. The corresponding conditioned mass is \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 17.1 supplies strip visit bounds independently of how the excursion exits. Lemma 17.2 converts them into first-length masses, and Lemma 17.3 supplies the positive normalizer. Proposition 15 makes optional \\(h\\log^2h\\) confinement negligible for both mass and first moment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.3 (Strip visit weights)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly over strip vertices, \\( A_{S_h}(v)\\le h^{1/12+o(1)}. \\) Uniformly at vertices at least \\(\\delta h\\) layers from both walls, \\( A_{S_h}(v)=h^{1/12+o(1)}. \\) The upper bound holds in every convex lateral …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 17. Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over strip vertices,\n\\[\nA_{S_h}(v)\\le h^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nUniformly at vertices at least \\(\\delta h\\) layers from both walls,\n\\[\nA_{S_h}(v)=h^{1/12+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe upper bound holds in every convex lateral truncation, including lateral endpoints; the lower bound holds if \\(v\\) also has distance at least \\(\\delta h\\) from every lateral side.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every enclosing polygon in the strip has interior disjoint from nonzero translates by a transverse pure lattice period \\(p\\omega\\), with \\(p\\asymp h\\). Lemma 13.3 removes radius \\(&gt;p^{1+\\alpha}\\) polygons at bounded exponential cost; Proposition 13 controls the remaining disk gas. Let \\(h\\to\\infty\\) for fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), then \\(\\alpha\\downarrow0\\), to get the uniform upper exponent. A disk of radius comparable to \\(h\\) inside all relevant boundaries gives the lower exponent.</p><p>Proposition 14 transfers these polygon bounds to visits in finite convex truncations. Let lateral width tend to infinity at fixed \\(h\\). Horizontal-endpoint excursions exhaust the strip excursions. Proposition 15 makes contributions from the \\(O(h)\\) distant side ports vanish at a fixed vertex, giving the full-strip assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "averaging with a length insertion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The fixed-source conditioned first-length mass is \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use finite convex lateral truncations \\(D\\) and the reach-\\(\\vartheta h\\) condition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fixed-source conditioned first-length mass is \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\). Total fixed-source first-length mass without conditioning is at most \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For finite \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{v\\in D}A_D(v)\n=\\sum_{\\gamma\\text{ ordered excursion in }D}\n|\\gamma|x_*^{|\\gamma|}.\n\\]\nDivide by horizontal width and let width tend to infinity at fixed \\(h\\). Lemma 17.1 bounds the limit above by \\(h^{13/12+o(1)}\\). A symmetric central band of order \\(h\\) layers, more than \\(\\vartheta h\\) from either wall, gives the same lower exponent, and every path visiting it satisfies the condition from either starting wall.</p><p>There are only \\(O(h)\\) side sources, each with bounded fixed-\\(h\\) length mass by Proposition 15; their width-normalized contribution vanishes. Each horizontal wall has one source per unit length. Translation invariance and top-bottom symmetry identify the limiting average with twice the fixed-source length mass. Restricting first to bounded range, then removing the restriction by exponential tails, justifies this identification.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Its reach-\\(\\vartheta h\\) mass is \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\).", "statement": "<p>Conditioned mixed mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed-source mixed ensemble of Proposition 17.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Its reach-\\(\\vartheta h\\) mass is \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Bridges provide the lower bound \\(b_h\\). Returning arches meeting the condition are bounded by the half-plane height tail at scale \\(\\vartheta h\\). Proposition 6 gives both exponents.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For an absolute \\(C\\), \\( J\\le C(J_{CC}+m). \\) Moreover, uniformly in \\(v\\), \\( J_{CC}\\le h^{3/4+o(1)}m+O(h^{-100}). \\)", "statement": "<p>Marked-tripod comparison of intersecting pairs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(v\\) in the central third of an \\(h\\)-layer strip \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sum over all horizontal boundary positions.</p><p class=\"li\">Count each bridge once, bottom-to-top, and let \\(m=m(v)\\) be bridge mass through \\(v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Count each same-side arch on either wall once, and let \\(u=u(v)\\) be arch mass through \\(v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\) be the product-weight sum of intersecting bridge–arch pairs with at least one member through \\(v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J_{CC}\\) be the analogous ordered bridge–bridge pair sum.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\theta=\\pi/8,\\quad k=\\cos\\theta,\\quad\n  c=\\cos3\\theta=\\sin\\theta,\\quad\n  p=e^{i\\theta},\\quad\n  g=\\cos2\\theta,\\quad \\phi=2g-1=c/k.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute \\(C\\),\n\\[\nJ\\le C(J_{CC}+m). \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nMoreover, uniformly in \\(v\\),\n\\[\nJ_{CC}\\le h^{3/4+o(1)}m+O(h^{-100}). \\tag{9.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 18.1–18.3 establish the marked-tripod pair comparison. Lemma 18.4 supplies the lateral-tail estimate for bridge–bridge pairs. These give (9.1) and (9.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The phased full-exit sum through \\(v\\) equals the sum of prefixes to \\(v\\).", "statement": "<p>Deletion, first contact, and absolute convergence.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>An active path has phase \\(e^{i\\sigma W}\\). A prefix to an obstacle stops before first attempted entry and omits its vertex weight.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The phased full-exit sum through \\(v\\) equals the sum of prefixes to \\(v\\). For disjoint obstacles \\(v,X\\), exits hitting both equal prefixes to first \\(X\\) having visited \\(v\\), plus prefixes to first \\(v\\) having visited \\(X\\). These identities and the anchored connected pair/tripod sums below are absolutely convergent at fixed \\(h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply deletion flux to \\(v,X,v\\cup X\\) and subtract. First-contact conventions preserve weights.</p><p>For convergence, project the strip injectively into a tilted cylinder with a pure period having a sufficiently large transverse shift. Fixed-width bridge weights decay exponentially in longitudinal span by Proposition 1. Any anchored walk has length \\(O_h(D+1)\\) in longitudinal span \\(D\\). Weak-bridge unfolding on both halves uses subexponentially many lists and sublinearly many joints, with total span at least \\(D\\). Positive weak bridges extend to cut lines with bounded endpoint costs and inherit exponential span decay. The remaining losses are subexponential. This yields absolute convergence with polynomial factors and for connected finite products anchored at one site. Equivalently, perform finite lateral truncations first.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( R_A=E_A+A-L_*, \\qquad R_C=E_C+Z-2kL_*. \\)", "statement": "<p>Marked-tripod contact equations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take boundary ports \\(a,b,c'\\), with \\(b&lt;c'\\) on one wall in positive boundary order and \\(a\\) on the other; sum both wall choices.</p><p class=\"li\">A tripod has three arms disjoint until their center, whose vertex weight is taken once.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_*\\) sum tripods containing \\(v\\), with common phase \\(e^{i\\sigma W_a}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define:\n  \\(A\\): spectator arch \\(b\\to c'\\) containing \\(v\\), active full exit from \\(a\\) hitting it;\n  \\(E_A\\): spectator arch avoiding \\(v\\), active full exit from \\(a\\) hitting both;\n  \\(R_A\\): same avoiding spectator, active prefix from \\(a\\) to \\(v\\) hitting it;\n  \\(Z_b,Z_{c'}\\): spectator bridge \\(c'\\to a\\), respectively \\(b\\to a\\), containing \\(v\\), active full exit from \\(b\\), respectively \\(c'\\), hitting it;\n  \\(E_C,R_C\\): spectator bridge avoiding \\(v\\), counted once, active full exit hitting both, respectively prefix to \\(v\\) hitting it, from any boundary source.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(Z=Z_b+Z_{c'}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nR_A=E_A+A-L_*,\n\\qquad\nR_C=E_C+Z-2kL_*.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Planarity fixes arm cyclic order. Since joining \\(b\\) or \\(c'\\) to \\(a\\) makes a bridge of turn zero, with center join turns \\(+\\pi/3,-\\pi/3\\),\n\\[\ne^{i\\sigma W_a}=p\\,e^{i\\sigma W_b}\n=\\bar p\\,e^{i\\sigma W_{c'}}.\n\\]\nDeletion turns a hit spectator into first-contact tripods. Thus \\(A,pZ_b,\\bar pZ_{c'}\\), in common phase, restrict \\(L_*\\) by excluding \\(v\\) exclusively on the corresponding open active arm. If the spectator avoids \\(v\\), the before-first-contact contribution is the complementary restriction. The both-obstacles identity of Lemma 18.1 gives the displayed equations. Coincident active/spectator endpoints in the bridge sums are consistent: there is no visit to \\(v\\) before first contact there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( P=M_BM_T-(k/x_*)m\\ge-(k/x_*)m. \\) Furthermore, \\( 2c\\,\\Re(A+E_A)+2P \\le\\phi\\,\\Re(E_C+Z), \\) and hence \\( 2c(1-\\phi)J \\le2\\phi J_{CC}+(2k/x_*)m. \\)", "statement": "<p>Opposite-prefix balance and positive pair comparison.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Lemma 18.2. Write \\(u=u_B+u_T\\), and\n\\[\nM_B=m+2cu_B,\\qquad M_T=m+2cu_T.\n\\]\nLet \\(P\\) be the real product sum of intersecting opposite-wall prefixes to \\(v\\), one conjugately phased.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nP=M_BM_T-(k/x_*)m\\ge-(k/x_*)m. \\tag{9.2}\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n2c\\,\\Re(A+E_A)+2P\n\\le\\phi\\,\\Re(E_C+Z),\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n2c(1-\\phi)J\n\\le2\\phi J_{CC}+(2k/x_*)m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Disjoint opposite-wall prefixes join at \\(v\\) to give each bridge through it once. Their product omits one \\(x_*\\), and arrival-turn difference is \\(\\pm\\pi/3\\), of real phase \\(k\\). Subtract them from the full product to obtain (9.2).</p><p>Grow an opposite active path in \\(S\\setminus\\{v\\}\\) against a conjugately phased prefix spectator; add the two choices of active wall and take real parts. Valid leaves hitting spectators have sum\n\\[\n\\Re(2cR_A+R_C)+2P.\n\\]\nAt first contact, opposite-wall shoulders form a bridge \\(\\lambda\\) avoiding \\(v\\); the suffix toward \\(v\\) uses the free third edge and omits weights at its start and at \\(v\\). The two active choices have phases adding to \\(2g e^{-i\\sigma W'}\\).</p><p>Let \\(H(S;v)\\) denote the real whole-boundary prefix sum to \\(v\\). The new-boundary suffix sum in \\(S-\\lambda\\) equals old-boundary hit prefixes minus\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\lambda=H(S;v)-H(S-\\lambda;v)\\ge0.\n\\]\nThe inequality follows from the positive nesting expansion in Proposition 14. The deleted bridge is boundary-connected, so \\(S-\\lambda\\) is filled. Take finite truncations first and use Lemma 18.1 for limits. Sum only connected hit and new-boundary terms, not disconnected boundary sums.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\Re(2cR_A+R_C)+2P\n=\n2g\\left[\\Re R_C-\\sum_{\\lambda\\not\\ni v}w(\\lambda)\\Delta_\\lambda\\right].\n\\]\nInsert Lemma 18.2. The \\(L_*\\)-terms cancel because \\(\\phi k=c\\), giving the stated inequality.</p><p>Keeping bridge active exits gives \\(\\Re(A+E_A)\\ge J\\); other exits have positive real weights. Allowing coincident spectator endpoints and combining both active orientations gives\n\\[\n\\Re(E_C+Z)\\le2cJ+2J_{CC}.\n\\]\nUse (9.2), then \\(\\phi&lt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( J_{CC}\\le CT_hb_hm+O(h^{-100}) \\le h^{3/4+o(1)}m+O(h^{-100}). \\)", "statement": "<p>Upper bound for intersecting bridge pairs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu_h\\) be the fixed-bottom-source unnormalized bridge-shape measure, of mass \\(b_h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(T_h=h\\log^2h\\), and let \\(X(\\beta)\\) be a shape's maximum lateral distance.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nJ_{CC}\\le CT_hb_hm+O(h^{-100})\n\\le h^{3/4+o(1)}m+O(h^{-100}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If both shapes have \\(X\\le T_h\\), a fixed marked bridge admits at most \\(CT_h\\) hitting translations of the other. Either member may contain \\(v\\), changing only the constant.</p><p>For the remainder, a fixed-source shape \\(\\beta_1\\) has at most \\(|\\beta_1|\\) translations putting a vertex at \\(v\\), and\n\\[\n|\\beta_1|\\le C(h+1)(X_1+1).\n\\]\nThe second shape has at most \\(C(X_1+X_2+1)\\) hitting translations. Thus\n\\[\nR_h\\le C(h+1)\n\\iint_{\\max(X_1,X_2)&gt;T_h}\n(1+X_1+X_2)^2\\,d\\mu_h(\\beta_1)d\\mu_h(\\beta_2).\n\\]\nProposition 15, tail integration, and \\(b_h\\le1\\) give\n\\[\nR_h\\le C(h+1)(T_h+h+1)^2e^{-cT_h/(h+1)}\n=O(h^{-100}).\n\\]\nFinally use Proposition 6.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Lemma 9.1 (Translated bridge hits), promoted to a proposition for the proof structure", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Uniformly over all such arches, \\( \\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\ge h^{3/4-\\epsilon-o(1)}. \\) In particular, \\( \\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\ge h^{3/4-o(1)} \\) uniformly in the exponent sense.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha\\) be any same-side arch meeting the central third of an \\(h\\)-layer strip.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\) be the total mass of bridges, summed over all bottom sources, intersecting \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over all such arches,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\ge h^{3/4-\\epsilon-o(1)}.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\ge h^{3/4-o(1)}\n\\]\nuniformly in the exponent sense.</p>", "proof": "<p>Reflect if necessary so that \\(\\alpha\\) is a bottom arch, translate its source to \\(0\\), and use its prefix to first crossing \\(r=\\lfloor h/4\\rfloor\\).</p><p>If some intermediate cut has two arch-prefix crossings separated by at least \\(W\\), every bridge shape has at least \\(W-O(1)\\) hitting translates. Sum the bridge crosscut's signed left/right indicator difference at the two points over integer translations; it telescopes to their integer separation. Each discrepancy forces intersection. This gives \\((W-O(1))b_h\\).</p><p>If some first-crossing displacement \\(a\\) of the arch has \\(|a|\\ge W\\), pair each bridge shape with reflection about its source. If their corresponding displacements are \\(d,-d\\), then\n\\[\n\\max(|a-d|,|a+d|)\\ge W.\n\\]\nLemma 19.1 supplies at least \\(W-O(1)\\) hitting translates for one member of the reflection pair; reflection preserves weights. Again obtain \\(cWb_h\\).</p><p>Otherwise every first-crossing displacement is less than \\(W\\), and same-cut crossing spread is less than \\(W\\). All arch-prefix crossings lie within \\(2W\\), and the full prefix lies within \\(2W+O(1)\\), since inside one layer the internal zigzag is monotone.</p><p>By Lemma 19.3, bridge mass at least \\(h^{-1/4-o(1)}\\) makes an excursion beyond \\(4W\\) before first crossing \\(r\\). Match that prescribed excursion point to the confined arch prefix using Lemma 19.1. Its contact-offset interval has length at least \\(2W-O(1)\\), giving order \\(W\\) integer hitting translates. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_h(\\alpha)\\ge Wh^{-1/4-o(1)}\n=h^{3/4-\\epsilon-o(1)}.\n\\]\nAll constants and errors are uniform in the arch. First let \\(h\\to\\infty\\) for fixed \\(\\epsilon\\), then \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a compact connected set of equal-height parameter pairs containing both endpoint pairs and projecting onto both full parameter intervals.", "statement": "<p>Equal-height contact intervals.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Take two polygonal prefixes from the bottom to first crossings of the same layer cut, with internal heights strictly between the two cut heights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a compact connected set of equal-height parameter pairs containing both endpoint pairs and projecting onto both full parameter intervals. Its horizontal-difference image is an interval of contact translations, containing initial and final offsets and an offset matching any prescribed point on either prefix.</p>", "proof": "<p>Triangulate the parameter square so that height difference is piecewise linear. Small positive regular level sets have exactly two boundary endpoints near the diagonal corners, joined by an arc: near extremal endpoints heights are strictly monotone, and compact interior intervals stay away from extremal heights. A Hausdorff-convergent subsequence of these compact arcs yields a connected zero-set subset containing both corners. Each coordinate projection is connected and contains \\(0,1\\), hence is all of \\([0,1]\\). Horizontal differences map it to an interval.</p><p>At integer translations, contact of honeycomb walks gives a common vertex. Contact at the intermediate cut lies in full lattice edges and also gives a shared vertex of the full walks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( e_j\\le Ce^{-ch/W}. \\)", "statement": "<p>Confined bridge pieces in a thin tube.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Put \\(W=h^{1-\\epsilon}\\). For \\(h/10\\le j\\le h/6\\), let \\(e_j\\) be mass of fixed-source \\(j\\)-bridges confined laterally within \\(4W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ne_j\\le Ce^{-ch/W}. \\tag{9.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Surround the width-\\(8W\\) confinement by a width-\\(O(W)\\) tube whose side walls alternate upward slanted tiling steps, with safety margin. Join horizontally at top and bottom. Its unwrapped positive boundary tangent angles lie in \\([0,2\\pi]\\): on the right they are \\(\\pi/3,2\\pi/3\\), on the left \\(4\\pi/3,5\\pi/3\\). Closing a bottom-source exit gives turn \\(B-\\pi\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]\\). Thus original exit real weights are positive and comparable despite nonconvexity.</p><p>Add either of the two slanted upward-normal cuts. Boundary-source cancellation still holds, and genuinely new exits lie in the convex wedge formed by the cut and the bottom half-plane, so their turns are fixed. Their lost sums lie on separated rays. Intercepts staggered by \\(O(W+1)\\) nest the lost events between reaching heights \\(s+C(W+1)\\) and \\(s\\). As in Proposition 15, this gives fixed-factor decay per \\(C(W+1)\\) height increment. Top exits include the confined \\(j\\)-bridges, proving (9.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\int N\\,d\\mu_h=h^{1/2+o(1)},\\qquad \\int N^2\\,d\\mu_h\\le h^{5/4+o(1)}, \\) and consequently \\( \\mu_h(N>0)\\ge h^{-1/4-o(1)}. \\) Every counted bridge has the lateral excursion before first crossing layer \\(r\\).", "statement": "<p>Mass of wide bridge prefixes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(W=h^{1-\\epsilon}\\), \\(r=\\lfloor h/4\\rfloor\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Under the fixed-source \\(h\\)-bridge measure \\(\\mu_h\\), let \\(N(\\gamma)\\) count layers \\(j\\in[\\lceil h/10\\rceil,\\lfloor h/6\\rfloor]\\) crossed exactly once for which the initial \\(j\\)-bridge reaches lateral distance greater than \\(4W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int N\\,d\\mu_h=h^{1/2+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\int N^2\\,d\\mu_h\\le h^{5/4+o(1)},\n\\]\nand consequently\n\\[\n\\mu_h(N&gt;0)\\ge h^{-1/4-o(1)}.\n\\]\nEvery counted bridge has the lateral excursion before first crossing layer \\(r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a single-crossing cut, bridge pieces occupy disjoint open slabs and weights multiply exactly, since ports carry no weight. Proposition 6 and Lemma 19.2 give\n\\[\n\\int N\\,d\\mu_h\n=\\sum_jb_{h-j}(b_j-e_j)=h^{1/2+o(1)}.\n\\]\nDropping lateral restrictions at two marked single-crossing cuts yields\n\\[\n\\int N^2\\,d\\mu_h\n\\le2\\sum_{j_1\\le j_2}\nb_{j_1}b_{j_2-j_1}b_{h-j_2}\n\\le h^{5/4+o(1)}.\n\\]\nUse \\(b_0=1\\), \\(\\sum_{d\\le h}b_d=h^{3/4+o(1)}\\), and outside factors \\(h^{-1/4+o(1)}\\), uniformly in the marked range. Unnormalized Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\mu_h(N&gt;0)\\ge\n\\frac{(\\int N\\,d\\mu_h)^2}{\\int N^2\\,d\\mu_h}.\n\\]\nA single crossing at \\(j&lt;r\\) forces the initial piece to occur before first crossing \\(r\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 24, "cite"], [4, 72, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 20, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [8, 22, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 12, "cite"], [16, 12, "cite"], [17, 34, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 3, "cite"], [21, 31, "cite"], [21, 51, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [23, 32, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 65, "cite"], [24, 70, "cite"], [24, 75, "cite"], [24, 79, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [25, 29, "cite"], [26, 24, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 24, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [28, 24, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 24, "cite"], [30, 24, "cite"], [31, 3, "cite"], [31, 37, "cite"], [31, 38, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 3, "cite"], [34, 45, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [36, 34, "cite"], [37, 3, "cite"], [37, 42, "cite"], [37, 45, "cite"], [38, 37, "cite"], [39, 37, "cite"], [39, 43, "cite"], [40, 37, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [41, 37, "cite"], [42, 3, "cite"], [42, 45, "cite"], [42, 50, "cite"], [43, 42, "cite"], [44, 42, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [45, 3, "cite"], [45, 51, "cite"], [45, 52, "cite"], [45, 53, "cite"], [46, 45, "cite"], [46, 48, "cite"], [47, 45, "cite"], [47, 48, "cite"], [48, 45, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [49, 45, "cite"], [50, 45, "cite"], [51, 3, "cite"], [52, 51, "cite"], [53, 51, "cite"], [54, 66, "cite"], [54, 68, "cite"], [55, 54, "cite"], [55, 57, "cite"], [56, 57, "cite"], [57, 54, "cite"], [57, 68, "cite"], [58, 66, "cite"], [58, 68, "cite"], [58, 74, "cite"], [59, 58, "cite"], [60, 58, "cite"], [61, 0, "cite"], [61, 2, "cite"], [61, 65, "cite"], [61, 67, "cite"], [61, 68, "cite"], [61, 69, "cite"], [61, 75, "cite"], [61, 78, "cite"], [62, 61, "cite"], [63, 61, "cite"], [64, 1, "cite"], [65, 64, "cite"], [66, 64, "cite"], [67, 0, "cite"], [67, 2, "cite"], [68, 67, "cite"], [68, 69, "cite"], [69, 67, "cite"], [70, 67, "cite"], [71, 0, "cite"], [72, 71, "cite"], [72, 73, "cite"], [72, 74, "cite"], [73, 71, "cite"], [73, 74, "cite"], [74, 71, "cite"], [75, 71, "cite"], [76, 0, "cite"], [77, 76, "cite"], [78, 79, "cite"], [79, 76, "cite"]]});
